/**
 * Inserts a "Character and Personality" content section into every Person
 * (character) article, placed immediately after "Birth and early life" (or after
 * the first/Overview section when no birth section exists). Content is hand-written
 * per person, source-aware, and never templated. Idempotent: re-running replaces
 * the existing Character and Personality section rather than duplicating it.
 */
import fs from 'node:fs'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'
import path from 'node:path'

const __dirname = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))
const dataPath = path.join(__dirname, '../server/data/history.json')
const data = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(dataPath, 'utf8'))

const SECTION_TITLE = 'Character and Personality'

// id -> array of paragraph strings
const content = {}
Object.assign(content, {
  'charlemagne': [
    'Charlemagne is one of the few early medieval rulers whose personality reaches us through a writer who knew the court closely. Einhard, in his Life of Charlemagne, drew on the model of Suetonius to present a king who was physically imposing, tirelessly energetic, fond of hunting and swimming, plain in his Frankish dress, and impatient with ceremony he considered foreign. That portrait is admiring and shaped by classical convention, but it agrees with what his reign shows: a ruler of enormous appetite for activity who treated conquest, church reform, and learning as a single project.',
    'Beneath the genial image Einhard offered was a hard and demanding ruler. The mass execution of Saxon captives at Verden, the decades-long war to force Saxon conversion, and his insistence on personal oversight of bishops and counts reveal a man who fused piety with coercion and rarely distinguished the two. He valued learning he could not fully share — Einhard notes he kept tablets under his pillow to practice writing late in life and never mastered it — and he gathered scholars such as Alcuin of York to a court he wanted to make the intellectual centre of the West.',
    'His remembered character is therefore double: the approachable, family-centred patriarch of court anecdote, and the relentless conqueror whose will reshaped Europe. Both belong to the same man, and later tradition — which turned him into a near-saint and a chivalric legend — tended to keep the first and soften the second.'
  ],
  'eleanor-of-aquitaine': [
    'Eleanor of Aquitaine\'s personality has been distorted by centuries of legend, much of it hostile and gendered. Clerical chroniclers of her own day, uneasy with a woman who wielded real power, circulated stories of scandal during the Second Crusade and presented her independence as waywardness. Modern historians treat these accounts cautiously: they reveal more about anxieties over female authority than about Eleanor herself.',
    'What the record more securely supports is a woman of formidable political intelligence and stamina. She held Aquitaine in her own right, survived the annulment of one royal marriage and the long imprisonment that followed her support for her sons\' revolt against Henry II, and re-emerged in old age as an active power-broker for Richard the Lionheart and John. Her endurance through decades of confinement and her decisive interventions in the succession show patience, resilience, and a refusal to be sidelined.',
    'Later tradition romanticised her as the queen of courtly love, presiding over "courts of love" in Poitiers — a picture that owes more to later literary imagination than to evidence. The vivid, scheming, glamorous Eleanor of legend should be held at arm\'s length; the politically tenacious and unusually long-lived duchess-queen is the figure the sources actually sustain.'
  ],
  'william-the-conqueror': [
    'William the Conqueror\'s character was remembered through severity and command. Norman sources such as William of Poitiers emphasised his legitimacy, discipline, and piety, while English memory — and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle — preserved the violence of conquest, dispossession, and above all the Harrying of the North, when he devastated whole regions to break resistance. The same chronicle that records his harshness also credits him with a fierce sense of order: "so stern and hot" a man that no one dared act against his will.',
    'His personality should not be reduced to cruelty alone. He was politically patient, careful with claims of law and inheritance, and capable of binding the Norman aristocracy to a long, disciplined project of occupation. He was personally pious in the conventional manner of his age, founding Battle Abbey on the site of Hastings, yet entirely willing to use terror as an instrument of rule when opposition hardened.',
    'Contemporaries also noted his physical formidableness and his growing corpulence in later life, the subject of a mocking jest by the king of France that William answered with a punitive campaign. The anecdote, whatever its truth, fits the remembered man: proud, unforgiving of insult, and relentless once provoked.'
  ],
  'hildegard-of-bingen': [
    'Hildegard of Bingen emerges from her own writings as a personality of striking conviction and intellectual range. She described herself with conventional humility as a "poor little figure of a woman," yet she corresponded with popes, emperors, and abbots, rebuked Frederick I Barbarossa, undertook preaching tours unusual for a woman of her age, and insisted that her visions came directly from "the Living Light." That combination of professed humility and absolute confidence in her divine authority is the key to how contemporaries experienced her.',
    'Her surviving letters and the testimony around her reveal someone determined, occasionally combative, and unwilling to yield on matters of principle — most famously in her late-life standoff with the clergy of Mainz over the burial of an excommunicate in her convent\'s ground, which she refused to surrender even under interdict. Alongside this iron will ran an extraordinary creativity: theology, music, medicine, and natural science all flowed from the same restless mind.',
    'Because so much comes from her own pen and from admirers cultivating her sanctity, the portrait is partly self-fashioned. But the consistency of the evidence — visionary certainty, administrative skill, and a refusal to be silenced — makes Hildegard one of the more vividly recoverable personalities of the twelfth century.'
  ],
  'saladin': [
    'Saladin\'s personality was shaped in memory by both Muslim and Latin writers, which makes him unusually complex. His own biographers, especially Baha al-Din ibn Shaddad and Imad al-Din, emphasised piety, justice, generosity, and Sunni legitimacy, presenting a ruler who wept at the Quran and gave away wealth until his treasury was nearly empty at his death. Later European tradition, remarkably, turned this enemy of the crusaders into a model of chivalric nobility.',
    'That admiration should not erase his political hardness. Saladin was a patient strategist who built power through family appointments, diplomacy, siege warfare, and ideological authority, and who could be ruthless — he executed Reynald of Châtillon with his own hand after the Battle of Hattin and ordered the killing of captured Templars and Hospitallers. His reputation for mercy, above all his restraint after the capture of Jerusalem in 1187, became central to his legend and contrasted deliberately, in the sources, with the massacre that had followed the First Crusade.',
    'The result is a figure who must be read critically: the generous, restrained, devout Saladin is real in the tradition, but he stands beside, not instead of, the calculating dynast who unified Egypt and Syria by force and persuasion. The chivalric myth that later European writers built around him tells us as much about their needs as about the man.'
  ],
  'richard-the-lionheart': [
    'Richard the Lionheart\'s remembered character is built around courage and martial brilliance, and even hostile or cautious sources concede his skill and personal bravery in war. Contemporaries on both sides of the Third Crusade described a commander of reckless physical daring and tactical command, whose reputation at Arsuf and Jaffa made him a figure of legend among Muslim and Christian writers alike. The byname "Lionheart" was already attaching to him in his lifetime.',
    'The same qualities carried obvious flaws. He was impulsive, quick to quarrel, and politically careless of the kingdom he ruled but rarely visited; his massacre of prisoners at Acre, his feuds with Philip II of France and Leopold of Austria, and the ransom that drained England after his capture all flowed from a temperament built for the battlefield rather than for patient governance. He spent only a few months of his reign in England and treated it largely as a source of money for war.',
    'Later medieval romance amplified him into the perfect crusading knight, and that heroic memory has proved durable. The historical Richard supports much of it — he genuinely was brave, charismatic, and a gifted soldier — but the legend smooths over the volatility and the cost of his single-minded pursuit of glory.'
  ],
  'joan-of-arc': [
    'Joan of Arc is one of the few medieval figures whose voice survives in something close to her own words, through the transcripts of her trial of condemnation in 1431. Those records, produced by hostile judges trying to destroy her, paradoxically preserve a personality of extraordinary force: defiant, quick-witted, and unshakeably certain of her mission. Her famous answer when asked whether she was in God\'s grace — a theological trap — has been admired for centuries as a flash of native intelligence under pressure.',
    'The Joan the sources show is devout, disciplined, and physically courageous, insistent on the propriety of her conduct among soldiers, contemptuous of hesitation, and charismatic enough to bend hardened commanders to a peasant girl\'s conviction. Her certainty about her voices — Saint Michael, Saint Catherine, Saint Margaret — was the core of her, and it never wavered even under the threat of burning.',
    'Her reputation has been pulled in many directions since: heretic, saint, national symbol, and modern icon. But the trial record, read carefully, anchors the personality firmly — a young woman of fierce religious conviction and remarkable presence of mind, whose confidence was inseparable from her belief that she acted on divine command.'
  ],
  'isabella-of-castile': [
    'Isabella I of Castile was remembered as pious, resolute, and politically formidable, a queen who governed in her own right and bound her authority tightly to religion. Contemporary admirers stressed her devotion, her personal austerity, and her tireless attention to the business of rule; she travelled constantly, dispensed justice in person, and was credited with restoring royal authority after a period of disorder. Her piety was genuine and severe, and it had a hard institutional edge.',
    'That same conviction drove the darker achievements of her reign: the establishment of the Spanish Inquisition under crown control, the conquest of Granada, and the 1492 expulsion of the Jews. These were not aberrations from her character but expressions of it — a fusion of religious zeal, dynastic ambition, and centralising will. She was politically shrewd in her marriage to Ferdinand of Aragon, careful to preserve Castile\'s independence within the partnership.',
    'Later Spanish national and Catholic memory idealised her as a model Christian queen, while modern assessment weighs the consolidation of Spanish power against the human cost of her religious policy. Both readings draw on the same traits: discipline, certainty, and an unbending sense of providential mission.'
  ],
  'mehmed-ii': [
    'Mehmed II combined intellectual curiosity with overwhelming imperial ambition, and the sources — Ottoman, Greek, and Italian — present a ruler who was as fascinated by knowledge as by conquest. He read history, was drawn to the legacy of Rome and Alexander, commissioned the Italian painter Gentile Bellini to portray him, and styled himself heir to the Roman imperial tradition after taking Constantinople in 1453. That self-image as a universal emperor shaped his entire reign.',
    'He was also ruthless and calculating to a degree that unsettled even his own court. He institutionalised fratricide for the sake of dynastic stability, drove his armies and engineers to extraordinary feats at the siege of Constantinople, and pursued relentless campaigns in the Balkans and Anatolia. Greek and Italian writers who feared him emphasised cruelty; Ottoman writers emphasised vision and justice. The truth the record supports is a ruler of cold strategic patience capable of sudden, decisive violence.',
    'His personality mattered directly to history: the fall of Constantinople owed much to his refusal to abandon a difficult siege, and his fusion of scholarly ambition with conquest set the cultural tone of the empire he transformed. He is best understood not as a simple tyrant but as a calculating, intellectually restless empire-builder.'
  ],
  'margaret-i': [
    'Margaret I of Denmark was remembered as patient, shrewd, and exceptionally skilled at the slow work of dynastic politics. Without ever holding a crown formally in her own name for most of her career, she assembled control of Denmark, Norway, and Sweden into the Kalmar Union through inheritance, marriage diplomacy, careful management of nobles, and the placement of a chosen heir, Eric of Pomerania. Contemporaries and later Scandinavian tradition credited her with a statecraft built on calculation rather than display.',
    'Her style was the opposite of the warrior-king ideal: she worked through negotiation, legal claims, and control of revenue and fortresses, defeating Albert of Mecklenburg and outmanoeuvring factions that underestimated a woman ruling in fact if not always in title. The nickname later attached to her — comparing her to a "lady king" — captures how unusual and effective her authority seemed.',
    'Because much of the admiring assessment comes from the success of her union and from later Danish national memory, it is worth noting that her methods could be coldly pragmatic and her control of the crowns rested on firm management of an often resentful aristocracy. The consistent picture, though, is of a ruler whose power came from intelligence, patience, and political subtlety rather than force.'
  ],
  'william-wallace': [
    'William Wallace\'s personality is hard to separate from the patriotic legend that grew around him, much of it shaped long after his death by the chronicle of Blind Harry in the fifteenth century. What the more contemporary record supports is a man of fierce resolve and uncompromising defiance: a figure of relatively modest birth who rose through sheer force of will and military boldness to lead resistance after the killing of an English sheriff, and who won a stunning victory at Stirling Bridge.',
    'English sources, hostile by nature, painted him as a brigand and a savage, while later Scottish memory made him the embodiment of national freedom. Both are propaganda of a kind. The defiance, however, is securely attested by his actions and by his refusal to submit even when his cause collapsed after Falkirk and he was hunted, captured, and subjected to a brutal execution as a traitor — a status he rejected on the grounds that he had never sworn loyalty to the English king.',
    'That final defiance, recorded even by his enemies, is the firmest evidence of his character: a man who would not bend, who chose resistance over accommodation, and whose intransigence made him a martyr and then a legend. The vivid personal detail belongs largely to later tradition; the unbreakable will belongs to the record.'
  ],
  'ragnar-lothbrok': [
    'Ragnar Lothbrok\'s personality belongs more to saga literature than to secure biography. In the Old Norse sagas and in later legend he is not merely a raider but a performer of dangerous confidence: boastful, cunning, theatrical, and eager to test himself against impossible odds, from slaying a great serpent to win a bride to facing death in a pit of snakes with a defiant death-song. That tradition is invaluable for understanding his legendary appeal, but it cannot be treated as a reliable psychological portrait of a single historical man.',
    'The saga Ragnar is a character built for entertainment and heroic memory — quick with a scheme, contemptuous of caution, and concerned above all with fame and the reputation of his sons. Whether any single Viking leader behind the legend possessed these traits is unknowable; the figure as we have him is a composite shaped by storytellers across generations.',
    'The honest approach is to describe how the legend characterises him rather than to invent a real personality from it. Ragnar embodies the Norse heroic ideal of the fearless, boastful adventurer who laughs at death — a literary creation whose vividness is precisely the point, and whose historical core remains beyond recovery.'
  ],
  'sigurd-of-norway': [
    'Sigurd the Crusader is remembered in the saga tradition, above all in Snorri Sturluson\'s Heimskringla, as a king of restless ambition and grand gestures, whose journey to the Holy Land gave him a prestige no earlier Norwegian ruler had claimed. The sagas present him as proud, magnificent, and conscious of his own standing, celebrated for leading a fleet to Jerusalem, fighting in Iberia and the Mediterranean, and being received with honour in Constantinople, where he reportedly left his ships to the Byzantine emperor.',
    'The same tradition, however, also preserves a darker strand: episodes of instability and harsh temper in his later years, including quarrels and moments of erratic behaviour that the sagas record with unusual frankness for a celebrated king. Because these accounts were written more than a century after his death, the specific incidents must be treated as saga memory rather than documented fact.',
    'What the tradition consistently conveys is a ruler defined by the crusading achievement that set him apart, combined with a pride and volatility that his admirers did not entirely conceal. The crusade made his reputation; the saga writers, working from oral memory, gave him a personality to match its grandeur and its strains.'
  ],
  'cnut-the-great': [
    'Cnut the Great was remembered as a pragmatic and adaptable ruler who governed a North Sea empire by knowing when to be ruthless and when to conciliate. He came to the English throne by conquest and secured it by a mixture of calculated violence — the early elimination of rivals — and deliberate reconciliation, ruling through English law and custom, endowing English churches, and presenting himself as a Christian king rather than a foreign conqueror. That political flexibility is the core of his remembered character.',
    'Later tradition softened him into a model of pious humility, most famously in the much-retold story of Cnut commanding the tide to stop — a tale told, in its earliest form, to show not arrogance but the opposite: a king demonstrating to flattering courtiers that earthly power was nothing before God. Whether or not the episode happened, its survival reflects how his reign was understood, as the rule of a powerful man who cultivated an image of Christian restraint.',
    'The sources, English and Scandinavian, broadly agree on a ruler who was both hard and shrewd: capable of the brutal logic of conquest yet politically intelligent enough to rule a composite empire by accommodation. His personality, as far as it can be recovered, was that of a calculating pragmatist who understood the value of legitimacy.'
  ],
  'alfred-the-great': [
    'Alfred\'s remembered character combines endurance with intellectual purpose, and unusually for an early medieval king it comes partly from a near-contemporary biography by the Welsh bishop Asser, as well as from Alfred\'s own prefaces to the works he had translated. Later tradition made him the learned king par excellence, but that reputation rests on real features of his reign: his concern for books, translation, education, law, and Christian kingship, and his stated grief at the decay of learning in England.',
    'He was also remembered as a ruler who survived disaster without letting it define him. The image of Alfred in the marshes at Athelney, regrouping after the near-collapse of Wessex before his victory at Edington, belongs partly to heroic memory — including the legend of the burnt cakes — but it captures something the record supports: patient, disciplined recovery after near-destruction. Asser also dwells on a recurring illness that troubled Alfred through his life, presenting his achievements as won against physical suffering.',
    'Because Asser was an admirer writing for a living patron, the portrait is idealised, and the burnt-cakes story is later folklore. But the consistent core — piety, a genuine and unusual commitment to learning, and resilience under pressure — is well grounded in Alfred\'s own writings and his documented reforms.'
  ],
  'sweyn-forkbeard': [
    'Sweyn Forkbeard is remembered, in both Scandinavian saga tradition and the hostile reports of English and German churchmen, as a hard and relentless war-leader. Adam of Bremen, writing from a clerical and often unfriendly standpoint, cast him as a rebel against his father Harald Bluetooth and treated his early career with suspicion, while the saga tradition emphasised his persistence and success as a raider and conqueror who finally seized England in 1013.',
    'The competing portraits reflect their sources: Christian writers uneasy about a king they associated with paganism and violence, against a Norse memory of a formidable and ultimately triumphant ruler. What both sustain is a personality defined by ambition and martial drive — a man who spent years harrying England before taking its throne, only to die within weeks of his triumph.',
    'Because the contemporary record is thin and shaped by partisanship, his inner character is largely inaccessible; what survives is reputation rather than intimate portrait. The figure the sources support is a determined, war-hardened dynast whose persistence achieved what had long eluded Scandinavian rulers.'
  ]
})

Object.assign(content, {
  'godfred-of-denmark': [
    'Godfred of Denmark is known almost entirely from Frankish sources, above all the Royal Frankish Annals, which present him as the great pagan adversary of Charlemagne on the empire\'s northern frontier. To these Christian imperial writers he was bold to the point of arrogance — a king who boasted he would march on Aachen itself, fortified the Danevirke rampart, raided Frisia, and exacted tribute, embodying the threat of an organised pagan power on the edge of Christendom.',
    'Because the portrait comes wholly from his enemies, it tells us how the Franks perceived a dangerous neighbour rather than how Godfred saw himself. What can be said with some confidence, from his documented actions, is that he was an ambitious and assertive ruler willing to confront the most powerful man in Europe. His murder by one of his own followers in 809 cut short the confrontation and left his character fixed, in the record, as the defiant heathen king who menaced Charlemagne.'
  ],
  'hemming-of-denmark': [
    'Hemming of Denmark appears only briefly in the Frankish annals, and his personality is essentially beyond recovery. What the record preserves is a single defining act: where his predecessor Godfred had threatened war with Charlemagne, Hemming made peace, concluding a treaty in 811 that fixed the frontier at the river Eider.',
    'From this the sources imply a more conciliatory and pragmatic ruler than the aggressive Godfred, though the impression rests on one decision rather than any rounded account. His reign was very short, ending with his death around 812, and the honest assessment is that he survives as a name attached to a peace treaty rather than as a recoverable character — a reminder of how thin the evidence becomes for early Danish kingship.'
  ],
  'horik-i-of-denmark': [
    'Horik I is one of the better-documented early Danish kings, appearing repeatedly in the Frankish annals across a long reign, yet his character remains only faintly visible. The sources show a ruler navigating relentless dynastic violence — he reached sole power after years of conflict among Godfred\'s heirs — and then managing a delicate relationship with the Frankish empire, alternately tolerating and restraining Viking raids on its coasts.',
    'He is also remembered for his contact with Christian mission: he received the missionary Ansgar with some favour, permitting church-building, without himself abandoning the old religion. This suggests a pragmatic ruler balancing pagan tradition, dynastic survival, and imperial diplomacy. He died around 854 in a fresh outbreak of family bloodshed that reportedly killed most of the royal house. The picture is of a long-surviving political operator in a murderous dynastic environment, though the inner man stays out of reach.'
  ],
  'horik-ii-of-denmark': [
    'Horik II succeeded to a Danish kingship shattered by the slaughter of nearly the entire royal family around 854, and he is among the most shadowy rulers in the archive. The Frankish sources note that he, too, dealt with the missionary Ansgar and at first acted against Christian activity before relenting and permitting it, which has left a faint impression of a ruler who could be persuaded and who tolerated the new religion under diplomatic pressure.',
    'Beyond this almost nothing survives. His personality cannot be reconstructed from the few notices that mention him, and the honest position is that he is known as a name in a fragile dynastic sequence rather than as a man whose temperament can be described. What little the record allows points to accommodation rather than confrontation with Christian mission and Frankish influence.'
  ],
  'gorm-the-old': [
    'Gorm the Old stands at the threshold between legend and history, remembered as the first king of a unified Denmark in the line that produced Harald Bluetooth. The most direct testimony to him is not a chronicle but a stone: the smaller Jelling runestone, which he raised in memory of his wife Thyra, calling her "Denmark\'s adornment." That single monument is the firmest trace of the man.',
    'Later tradition, especially Saxo Grammaticus and saga material, attached stories to him — a stubborn attachment to the old gods, a famous grief at the death of his son — but these are late and unreliable. What the runestone and the Jelling complex genuinely convey is a ruler concerned with dynasty, memory, and the marking of royal status in stone. His epithet "the Old" reflects later memory of him as a founding ancestor rather than any documented quality of character; the historical Gorm remains largely a figure of monument and tradition.'
  ],
  'harald-bluetooth': [
    'Harald Bluetooth is one of the few tenth-century Scandinavian kings to leave a direct statement of how he wished to be remembered, carved on the great Jelling runestone: the king "who won for himself all Denmark and Norway and made the Danes Christian." That inscription reveals a ruler intensely conscious of legacy, presenting himself as both unifier and Christianiser in a single monumental claim.',
    'The archaeological record — the ring fortresses, the Jelling complex, the bridge at Ravning — supports the image of an ambitious organiser who mobilised resources on an impressive scale. Yet the saga and chronicle tradition also remembers him as a king undone by his own son: Sweyn Forkbeard rebelled against him, and Harald died of wounds from that conflict. The German chronicler Adam of Bremen and later Norse memory frame him variously as pious convert and as a ruler whose conversion was a calculated political move under Saxon pressure.',
    'The honest reading balances his own grand self-presentation against the messier tradition of a reign that ended in defeat by his heir. The consistent core is ambition expressed through unification, conversion, and monumental building — a king who wanted, above all, to be remembered as the maker of Christian Denmark.'
  ],
  'harthacnut': [
    'Harthacnut, son of Cnut the Great, is remembered with notable hostility in the English sources, which is most of what survives of his character. The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle treats his short reign coldly, recording his imposition of a heavy and resented fleet-tax and, most damningly, his posthumous vengeance on his half-brother Harold Harefoot, whose body he had exhumed and thrown into a fen.',
    'That act of desecration, together with a reputation for harsh taxation, fixed him in English memory as a graceless and vindictive ruler, in contrast to his formidable father. He is also recorded as dying suddenly at a wedding feast, "as he stood at his drink," which did nothing to soften the unflattering portrait.',
    'Because the record is brief and largely English — that is, written by those who paid his taxes and disliked his Danish rule — the assessment is one-sided. What it consistently conveys is a ruler remembered for severity and resentment rather than achievement, his character preserved mainly through the grievances of those he governed.'
  ],
  'magnus-the-good': [
    'Magnus the Good earned his epithet in the saga tradition, where he is presented as a king who learned to temper early harshness into justice and generosity. Snorri Sturluson\'s Heimskringla tells how the young Magnus, returning to Norway, at first pursued vengeance against the families of his father Olaf II\'s enemies, until a counsellor warned him with a poem; chastened, he is said to have softened into the fair and beloved ruler the byname commemorates.',
    'That narrative is moral and didactic, shaped to teach the lesson of kingly restraint, so the neat arc of reform must be read as saga literature rather than documented psychology. Still, the tradition consistently presents Magnus as capable of growth and as broadly admired, ruling both Norway and Denmark and remembered fondly in both.',
    'What survives, then, is a reputation more than a fully recoverable personality: the "Good" king of Norse memory, whose story was told to illustrate the value of mercy in a ruler. The historical Magnus is glimpsed through that improving lens, which makes the goodness as much a literary judgement as a biographical fact.'
  ],
  'sweyn-ii-estridsson': [
    'Sweyn II Estridsson is unusually well lit for an eleventh-century Danish king because Adam of Bremen knew him personally and drew on him as a source, leaving a portrait coloured by direct acquaintance. Adam presents a learned, talkative, and devout king, deeply interested in the history of his own people and of the north, who supplied the chronicler with information and impressed him with his knowledge — though Adam also disapproved of his irregular marriages and the resulting friction with the church.',
    'This gives a rare double impression: a ruler genuinely engaged with learning and the church, generous to Adam\'s informants, yet stubborn in personal matters the clergy found scandalous. He fought a long and ultimately unsuccessful war against Harald Hardrada and founded a dynasty through his many sons, several of whom became kings in turn.',
    'Because the fullest portrait comes from a churchman who both admired and criticised him, the character is more rounded than for most of his predecessors: pious and curious, politically resilient after repeated military setbacks, and independent enough to defy ecclesiastical disapproval over his marriages.'
  ],
  'valdemar-i-of-denmark': [
    'Valdemar I of Denmark, called the Great, emerged from a long and bloody civil war and is remembered as the ruler who restored strong royal authority to Denmark. Saxo Grammaticus, writing under Valdemar\'s dynasty, gave him a heroic and partisan treatment, presenting him as the decisive victor who ended the chaos of rival claimants and, with his close ally Bishop Absalon, launched campaigns against the Wends of the Baltic.',
    'Because Saxo wrote to glorify the Valdemarian line, the portrait is celebratory and must be read with that bias in mind. What the broader record supports is a determined and effective ruler who consolidated power after winning the throne through victory over his rivals, and who turned Danish energy outward into Baltic conquest and crusading. His partnership with Absalon shaped both his policy and his favourable image.',
    'The consistent picture is of a king defined by the restoration of order and the projection of power, remembered through a dynastic history that had every reason to admire him. The hard ambition that carried him through civil war into aggressive expansion is clear; the warmth Saxo adds belongs to the propaganda of his own house.'
  ],
  'cnut-vi-of-denmark': [
    'Cnut VI of Denmark ruled at the height of Danish Baltic power, and Saxo Grammaticus — who wrote during his reign — presents him favourably as a worthy heir to his father Valdemar I, continuing the expansion of royal and Danish authority. He is remembered chiefly for a single proud gesture of policy: his refusal to do homage to the Holy Roman Emperor, asserting Danish independence from imperial overlordship.',
    'That refusal, and the successful campaigns against the Wends and in the southern Baltic carried out largely by his brother and by Bishop Absalon, fixed his reputation as a king who upheld Danish sovereignty and continued his father\'s work. Saxo\'s closeness to the dynasty again means the portrait is partisan.',
    'Beyond his assertion of independence and the military successes of his reign, little of his personal temperament is recorded; he survives more through the achievements credited to his rule than through any vivid individual character. The defining note is dynastic continuity and a proud insistence on Danish autonomy.'
  ],
  'valdemar-ii-of-denmark': [
    'Valdemar II of Denmark, called the Victorious, is remembered for both the height and the collapse of Danish Baltic dominance, and his reputation carries the drama of that reversal. He extended Danish power across the southern Baltic and into Estonia — Danish tradition tied the origin of the flag, the Dannebrog, to his crusade there — and codified law in the Code of Jutland, marking him as both conqueror and lawgiver.',
    'The same reign was scarred by a humiliating episode that the sources dwell on: his capture by a vassal, Count Henry of Schwerin, during a hunting trip, and the long, costly captivity that followed, which shattered much of his Baltic empire. This combination of triumph and captivity gives his memory an unusual shape — the victorious king brought low by treachery and ransom.',
    'What the record supports is an energetic and ambitious ruler, effective as both warrior and legislator, whose personal misfortune became central to how his reign was remembered. The epithet "Victorious" sits, pointedly, beside the disaster of his captivity, and the tradition preserves both without fully reconciling them.'
  ],
  'eric-iv-of-denmark': [
    'Eric IV of Denmark, nicknamed Ploughpenny after a hated tax he is said to have imposed, is remembered through the bitter dynastic strife of his reign and a violent death. The sources present a king locked in relentless war with his own brothers over the division of the realm, and the tax-nickname suggests a ruler resented for fiscal demands.',
    'His reign ended in murder: he was killed, by tradition at the hands of agents of his brother Abel, his body reportedly thrown into a fjord. The fratricidal context — Abel swore an oath denying involvement that few believed — gave the episode a dark notoriety in Danish memory.',
    'Little survives of Eric\'s personality beyond the resentment captured in his nickname and the impression of a king consumed by family conflict. He is remembered less as an individual than as a figure in a violent dynastic tragedy, the victim of a brother\'s ambition, and the unpopular tax fixed to his name is most of the personal trace that endures.'
  ],
  'christopher-i-of-denmark': [
    'Christopher I of Denmark came to the throne after the murder of one brother and the death of another in the civil wars that defined his family, and he is remembered chiefly for a bitter conflict with the church. His struggle with Archbishop Jakob Erlandsen over royal and ecclesiastical authority dominated his reign and left him, in the clerical sources, as an opponent of the church\'s liberties.',
    'Those sources are naturally hostile, and they shape much of the surviving impression — a king at war with a powerful archbishop, dying suddenly in 1259 amid the conflict, with later tradition even suggesting he was poisoned by a cleric. The partisanship of the record makes his personal character hard to assess fairly.',
    'What can be said is that he was a ruler willing to confront the church directly in defence of royal power, in the combative tradition of his dynasty, and that he governed in an atmosphere of continual conflict. The man behind the disputes is faintly drawn; the reputation is that of a king defined by his quarrel with ecclesiastical authority.'
  ],
  'eric-v-of-denmark': [
    'Eric V of Denmark, called Klipping, is remembered above all through a famous constitutional concession and a famous murder. Pressed by a powerful and resentful nobility, he was forced in 1282 to grant a charter — often called Denmark\'s first — that bound the king to summon assemblies and respect law, a humiliation for royal authority that reflects a reign of weakness against the magnates.',
    'His death sealed his notoriety: he was killed in 1286, stabbed many times in a barn at Finderup, in one of the most sensational political murders of the Scandinavian Middle Ages. Suspicion fell on leading nobles, and the unsolved killing became a lasting subject of ballad and tradition.',
    'The sources convey a king who could not master his aristocracy and who met a violent end, but they preserve little of his individual temperament. He survives as a figure of royal weakness and dramatic murder rather than as a rounded personality, his reign remembered for the charter forced upon him and the assassination that ended it.'
  ],
  'christopher-ii-of-denmark': [
    'Christopher II of Denmark is remembered as one of the most unfortunate of Danish kings, presiding over a near-dissolution of the realm. The sources record a ruler repeatedly deposed and restored, forced into humiliating charters, and ultimately so indebted that much of Denmark was mortgaged to German counts, leaving the crown almost powerless by his death in 1332.',
    'This catalogue of failure shapes his reputation as a weak and luckless king, broken by the same noble factions and financial pressures that had undermined his predecessors. Whether the failures reflect personal incapacity or simply an impossible inheritance is hard to judge from the record, which dwells on events rather than character.',
    'What survives is the impression of a ruler overwhelmed by forces he could not control, ending his reign with the kingdom pawned and the monarchy in eclipse. His personality is largely lost behind the story of collapse; he is remembered as the king under whom Danish royal power reached its lowest point before its later recovery.'
  ],
  'valdemar-iv-atterdag': [
    'Valdemar IV of Denmark, called Atterdag, is remembered as the cunning restorer of a kingdom that had nearly ceased to exist, and the tradition credits him with exceptional political guile. Inheriting a realm mortgaged to foreign creditors, he reassembled it piece by piece through purchase, marriage, war, and ruthless opportunism, earning a reputation as a wily and relentless operator who recovered Danish royal power almost from nothing.',
    'His methods could be hard and provocative: his sack of Visby on Gotland in 1361 was long remembered for its severity, and his aggressive policies drew the Hanseatic League and Scandinavian rivals into war against him. The byname Atterdag — variously explained as "new day" or "again day" — came to symbolise the dawn he was felt to have brought to Danish fortunes.',
    'The consistent portrait is of a shrewd, determined, and sometimes unscrupulous ruler whose intelligence and persistence rebuilt the monarchy. He is remembered for statecraft rather than warmth — a calculating king who out-thought his enemies and laid the groundwork that his daughter Margaret I would extend into the Kalmar Union.'
  ],
  'eric-of-pomerania': [
    'Eric of Pomerania, raised to rule the united Kalmar Union by his great-aunt Margaret I, is remembered as a ruler whose ambition outran his political skill. While Margaret lived he governed in her shadow; after her death he pursued aggressive policies — above all a long war against the Holstein counts and the Hanseatic League over Schleswig — that drained the union and turned its constituent realms against him.',
    'His introduction of the Sound Dues, taxing shipping through the Øresund, showed a ruler alert to royal revenue, but his obstinacy and unpopular reliance on foreign officials alienated the Swedish and Danish nobility alike. Eventually deposed in all three kingdoms, he withdrew to Gotland, where tradition remembers him turning to piracy — a striking final image for a former union monarch.',
    'The sources, shaped partly by the nobles who rejected him, present a stubborn and ultimately unsuccessful king, capable in matters of revenue but politically inflexible. He survives as a cautionary figure: the heir to Margaret\'s grand construction who lacked her subtlety and lived to see the union turn against him.'
  ],
  'christopher-of-bavaria': [
    'Christopher of Bavaria, elected to the three Scandinavian thrones after the deposition of Eric of Pomerania, is remembered as a compromise king whose authority depended heavily on the nobility that chose him. The sources present a ruler who had to conciliate the powerful magnates and councils of Denmark, Sweden, and Norway, and a Danish nickname mocking him as the "Bark King," from a famine in his reign when people were said to mix bark into bread, fixed an unflattering note to his memory.',
    'His short reign is recorded more for its constitutional concessions and its dependence on aristocratic support than for any forceful royal initiative. He died suddenly and young in 1448 without an heir, reopening the contest for the union crowns.',
    'Little of his personal character survives beyond the impression of a careful, accommodating ruler constrained by those who had elected him. He is remembered as a transitional figure — a foreign-born compromise monarch whose brief reign left the union\'s future unresolved.'
  ],
  'christian-i-of-denmark': [
    'Christian I of Denmark, founder of the Oldenburg dynasty, is remembered as an ambitious but financially overstretched ruler who gathered crowns more easily than he could pay for them. Elected to Denmark, Norway, and for a time Sweden, he pursued a grand dynastic vision, but his reign is marked by chronic debt — most famously the pledging of Orkney and Shetland to Scotland as part of his daughter\'s dowry, a temporary expedient that became permanent.',
    'The sources present a king of large ambitions and limited means, constantly negotiating with creditors and a demanding nobility, and unable to hold Sweden securely within the union. He founded the University of Copenhagen and worked to raise the prestige of his new dynasty, showing genuine concern for its standing.',
    'What survives is the picture of a striving founder-king, more notable for dynastic ambition and financial difficulty than for any vivid personal trait. He is remembered as the originator of a durable royal house whose reach, in his own reign, repeatedly exceeded its resources.'
  ]
})

Object.assign(content, {
  'harald-fairhair': [
    'Harald Fairhair stands at the legendary origin of a unified Norway, and his personality, as we have it, is almost entirely a creation of the saga tradition written centuries later. Snorri Sturluson\'s Heimskringla gives him a famous motivating passion: a vow, made to win the proud woman Gyda, that he would not cut or comb his hair until he had made himself king of all Norway — a romantic explanation for his epithet and for the relentless campaign of unification credited to him.',
    'That story is patriotic legend, shaped to give Norway a single heroic founder, and the historical Harald is correspondingly hard to recover. The saga character is determined, proud, and single-minded, his long uncut hair a symbol of an oath pursued to the end, after which it was cut and he was renamed "Fairhair."',
    'The honest approach is to treat this as the way later Icelanders and Norwegians chose to imagine their kingdom\'s beginning rather than as biography. What the tradition consistently conveys is the ideal of the unifier-king — fixed, ambitious, and bound by a vow — a personality designed for a founding myth more than recovered from contemporary record.'
  ],
  'eric-bloodaxe': [
    'Eric Bloodaxe carries one of the most fearsome bynames in Norse tradition, and the saga literature lives up to it, presenting him as a violent and ruthless figure whose nickname was explained by the killing of several of his own brothers in the struggle for power. In the sagas he is overshadowed by his formidable and reputedly cruel wife Gunnhild, and the pair are cast as harsh, grasping rulers whose unpopularity drove him from Norway.',
    'His later career as a Viking king of Northumbria, ending in his death at Stainmore around 954, made him a figure of both Norse and English memory. The tradition is hostile and dramatic, shaped by storytellers who relished a brutal protagonist, so the specific atrocities attributed to him belong to saga colouring rather than secure fact.',
    'What the byname and the consistent direction of the tradition convey is a reputation for violence and ambition extreme even by the standards of the age — a king remembered as bloody-handed and overbearing. The literary Eric is vivid precisely because he is drawn as a villain; the historical man behind the legend is far harder to see.'
  ],
  'haakon-the-good': [
    'Haakon the Good is remembered in the saga tradition as a ruler caught between two worlds, and his character is built around that tension. Fostered as a Christian at the court of Æthelstan in England, he returned to a pagan Norway and is portrayed by Snorri Sturluson as genuinely devout in his new faith yet repeatedly forced to compromise with the powerful pagan farmers — most memorably compelled, at the Frostathing assemblies, to take part in heathen sacrifice against his conscience.',
    'This image of a good-natured, popular king, sincere in his Christianity but unable to impose it, gives him a sympathetic and somewhat tragic cast in the tradition. His epithet reflects a reputation for fairness and for the law-reforms credited to him, including the organisation of coastal defence.',
    'Because the portrait comes from Christian saga writers two centuries later, the religious drama is shaped to their purposes, and the precise episodes cannot be taken as documentary. The consistent memory, though, is of a mild, well-liked, and conscientious ruler whose personal faith ran ahead of what his society would accept — remembered for goodness rather than for force.'
  ],
  'olaf-tryggvason': [
    'Olaf Tryggvason is one of the most theatrical figures in the saga tradition, presented as a charismatic, physically magnificent, and forceful king who imposed Christianity on Norway with a heavy hand. Snorri Sturluson\'s account dwells on his athletic prowess, his commanding presence, and his zeal as a converter — a zeal that in the sagas shades into coercion, with stubborn pagans facing torture, exile, or death if they refused baptism.',
    'His end at the sea-battle of Svolder, where he is said to have leapt overboard rather than be taken, gave him a heroic and legendary death that storytellers embellished, including tales that he had survived and was seen again in the east. These flourishes mark how far his figure passed into legend.',
    'The saga Olaf is a compound of admiration and drama, written by Christians who celebrated his missionary role while not concealing its violence. What the tradition consistently conveys is a magnetic, headstrong, and aggressive king — converter and warrior — whose vivid personality owes much to the storytellers who made him a hero of Norway\'s Christianisation.'
  ],
  'olaf-ii-haraldsson': [
    'Olaf II Haraldsson became Saint Olaf, and his remembered character is shaped decisively by that transformation. In life, as the saga tradition presents him, he was a hard and determined ruler who pushed Christianity and royal authority on a resistant Norway with methods harsh enough to turn many chieftains against him, leading to his exile and his death at the Battle of Stiklestad in 1030.',
    'After death he was remembered very differently: reports of miracles turned the demanding, sometimes ruthless king into Norway\'s eternal patron saint, Rex Perpetuus Norvegiae. The hagiographic tradition emphasised piety, justice, and martyrdom, smoothing the severity that the same sources record from his reign.',
    'This double memory is the key to his personality as it survives: a forceful, uncompromising Christianiser whose hardness made enemies, reinterpreted after Stiklestad as holy zeal. The saint\'s legend and the king\'s harshness come from overlapping traditions, and an honest portrait holds both — the stern ruler and the venerated martyr he became.'
  ],
  'harald-hardrada': [
    'Harald Hardrada\'s reputation is one of hard ambition, and his very byname — "hard ruler" or "hard counsel" — encodes it. Saga tradition, above all Snorri Sturluson\'s Heimskringla, presents a ruler shaped by exile, mercenary service in the Byzantine Varangian Guard, and a relentless appetite for power. His years in Rus\' and in Byzantine service, fighting from the Mediterranean to the Holy Land, gave him the aura of a world-travelled warrior unlike any other northern king.',
    'The same tradition remembers him as severe, calculating, and dangerous to oppose, willing to crush Norwegian chieftains and to wage long, grinding wars against Denmark. He was also remembered as cultured in the Norse manner — a composer of skaldic verse even on the eve of battle — which sat beside, not against, his ruthlessness.',
    'In Norway that hardness made him a formidable king; in England it carried him into the gamble that ended at the Battle of Stamford Bridge in 1066, where he was killed reaching for a throne that was not his. The consistent saga portrait — ambitious, ruthless, charismatic, and battle-hardened — is vivid, though shaped by writers two centuries later who admired the grand, dangerous figure they remembered.'
  ],
  'magnus-barefoot': [
    'Magnus Barefoot is remembered in the saga tradition as a restless and aggressive warrior-king who looked outward to the western seas, reviving Norwegian power in the Hebrides, Man, and the Irish Sea. Snorri Sturluson presents him as a ruler who declared that a king was made for glory, not for long life — a credo that fits a reign of near-continuous campaigning and an early death in an ambush in Ulster in 1103.',
    'His byname is usually explained by his adoption of the Gaelic dress he encountered on his western expeditions, which the sagas record with some amusement, suggesting a king willing to take on the style of the lands he raided. The tradition emphasises boldness, energy, and a thirst for martial reputation over caution.',
    'Because the portrait is saga literature, the memorable sayings and details belong to storytelling more than to record, but the direction is consistent: an ambitious, war-loving king who spent his reign in motion and died young in battle. He is remembered as the last great Norwegian Viking-style adventurer, glory-seeking to the end.'
  ],
  'oystein-i-of-norway': [
    'Øystein I of Norway is remembered, unusually, as a builder rather than a warrior, and the saga tradition draws a deliberate contrast between him and his crusading brother Sigurd. Snorri Sturluson preserves a famous exchange — a "comparison of kings" — in which Sigurd boasts of his foreign campaigns and Øystein answers by listing the harbours, churches, roads, and laws he provided at home, arguing that such works served the people more lastingly than distant glory.',
    'Whether or not the dialogue is authentic, it captures how Øystein was remembered: as the conscientious, domestically minded ruler whose contribution lay in governance, infrastructure, and law rather than conquest. The tradition presents him as wise, eloquent, and concerned with the practical welfare of Norway.',
    'This is a personality preserved through a literary set-piece designed to weigh two models of kingship, so it must be read as saga argument as much as biography. Still, the consistent memory of Øystein is of a thoughtful, constructive king — the brother who built and administered while Sigurd sailed to Jerusalem.'
  ],
  'sverre-sigurdsson': [
    'Sverre Sigurdsson is one of the few medieval Norwegian kings whose character can be approached through a near-contemporary source close to him — Sverris saga, begun under his own supervision. That work, and his bitter struggle against the church and the established nobility, present a brilliant, combative, and self-made ruler who rose from obscure and disputed origins to seize the throne at the head of the Birkebeiner faction.',
    'He emerges as a gifted speaker, a daring military improviser, and a fierce polemicist who defended his kingship against papal excommunication with arguments of his own, even sponsoring a tract attacking clerical pretensions. His enemies in the church saw an unscrupulous usurper; the saga he shaped saw a providentially favoured leader of extraordinary cunning and resolve.',
    'Because the fullest portrait is one he helped author, it is partisan in his favour, and his claimed royal descent was always contested. But the consistent impression — eloquent, audacious, intellectually combative, and relentlessly determined — is well grounded in the record of a reign spent fighting both rival claimants and the institutional church.'
  ],
  'haakon-iv-haakonsson': [
    'Haakon IV Haakonsson presided over what later Norwegians called the kingdom\'s golden age, and he is remembered as a ruler who turned the violence of the civil-war era into stable, ambitious kingship. The contemporary saga written about him by Sturla Þórðarson presents a dignified, cultured, and politically astute monarch, conscious of royal majesty, who brought Iceland and Greenland under the Norwegian crown and corresponded with European rulers as an equal.',
    'His court embraced European chivalric culture, commissioning translations of romances, and his reign emphasised law, order, and the legitimacy of hereditary monarchy after generations of disputed succession — fitting for a king whose own birth had been contested. He died in Orkney in 1263 after the Scottish campaign that ended at Largs.',
    'Because the main saga was commissioned by the dynasty, its admiration is partisan, but the broad picture is well supported: a stabilising, status-conscious, and outward-looking ruler who raised Norway\'s standing. He is remembered for dignity and consolidation rather than for personal drama — the king who made the monarchy secure.'
  ],
  'magnus-vi-lawmender': [
    'Magnus VI of Norway earned the rare and revealing epithet "Lawmender" (Lagabøte), and it defines his remembered character more precisely than any anecdote. He is remembered as a peaceable, methodical ruler whose great achievement was a single national law code for Norway, replacing the older regional laws — an unusual and forward-looking act of legal unification for thirteenth-century Europe.',
    'The tradition presents him as conciliatory and prudent, preferring negotiation to war: he settled the long dispute with Scotland over the Hebrides and Man by the Treaty of Perth, accepting compensation rather than pursuing costly conflict, and worked to define the relationship between crown and church by agreement.',
    'His personality survives mainly through the consistent tenor of his reign — orderly, legalistic, and pacific — rather than through vivid personal report. He is remembered as the lawgiver-king whose temperament favoured stability and codified justice, a deliberate contrast to the warrior ideal, and his byname is the clearest testimony to how his contemporaries valued him.'
  ],
  'eric-ii-of-norway': [
    'Eric II of Norway, called the Priest-Hater, carries a byname that captures the single most remembered feature of his reign: a sustained conflict with the Norwegian church over royal rights and clerical privileges. The sources present a king, young when he came to the throne, drawn into bitter disputes with bishops that defined his public reputation as an opponent of ecclesiastical power.',
    'His reign is also remembered for his marriage to Margaret of Scotland and for the tragic fate of their daughter, the "Maid of Norway," whose death on the voyage to claim the Scottish throne reshaped the politics of the British Isles. These dynastic episodes loom larger in the record than any account of his temperament.',
    'Little survives of Eric\'s individual character beyond the anticlerical reputation fixed in his nickname and the impression of a reign shaped by others around him. He is remembered chiefly through his quarrel with the church and his place in the Scottish succession crisis rather than as a fully drawn personality.'
  ],
  'haakon-v-magnusson': [
    'Haakon V Magnusson is remembered as a centralising and cautious ruler who worked to strengthen royal authority against the high nobility, and the record of his reign supports that consistent direction. He is associated with moving the kingdom\'s administrative weight toward Oslo, building the fortress of Akershus, and restricting the power of the great lords — measures that suggest a deliberate, institutionally minded approach to kingship.',
    'His reign closed the line of Norwegian kings descended directly from the old dynasty in the male line; lacking a son, he arranged the succession through his daughter, leading to the union with Sweden under his grandson. This concern with orderly succession and the curbing of aristocratic rivals fits the picture of a careful, state-building monarch.',
    'The personal man is faintly drawn, but the policies are clear enough to characterise the reign: distrust of overmighty nobles, attention to fortification and administration, and a preference for strengthening the crown\'s institutions. He is remembered as a prudent consolidator at the end of an era rather than as a vivid individual.'
  ],
  'haakon-vi-of-norway': [
    'Haakon VI of Norway is remembered less for a strongly drawn personality than for his pivotal place in Scandinavian dynastic politics, as the last king to rule Norway as a fully independent realm before the unions. The sources present a ruler engaged in the complex contests over the Norwegian and Swedish crowns, and above all for his marriage to Margaret I of Denmark — a union that would shape the entire future of the north.',
    'His reign was spent navigating rivalry with the Mecklenburg claimants in Sweden and maintaining Norwegian autonomy under pressure, tasks that the record describes more in terms of events than of character. Through his son Olaf and his formidable wife, his dynastic line led directly to the Kalmar Union.',
    'What survives is the impression of a ruler defined by his position in a dynastic web rather than by recorded temperament. He is remembered chiefly as Margaret\'s husband and as the last independent Norwegian king, his individual character largely overshadowed by the union politics he helped set in motion.'
  ],
  'olaf-iv-of-norway': [
    'Olaf IV of Norway died at about seventeen, and almost nothing of a personal character can be recovered from so brief and so closely supervised a life. Born to Haakon VI and Margaret I, he was a child-king in whose name his mother governed, and the record concerns his dynastic significance — his claims to Norway, Denmark, and, through his father, to Sweden — rather than any individual temperament.',
    'His sudden death in 1387 was the hinge on which Scandinavian history turned, since it freed Margaret to consolidate power directly and move toward the Kalmar Union. A strange afterlife followed: a pretender later appeared in the Baltic claiming to be the supposedly dead Olaf, an episode that says more about the uncertainty around his death than about the boy himself.',
    'The honest assessment is that Olaf is a figure of dynastic potential cut short, not a recoverable personality. He is remembered for the consequences of his birth and early death rather than for anything he did or was said to be, his short life lived entirely within his mother\'s political design.'
  ]
})

Object.assign(content, {
  'eric-the-victorious': [
    'Eric the Victorious is among the earliest Swedish kings to step even partly out of legend, and his byname is almost the whole of his remembered character. The sources — late and largely Norse, including Adam of Bremen and saga material — credit him with a decisive victory over a rival, traditionally his own nephew Styrbjörn at the Battle of Fýrisvellir, which gave him both his throne and his epithet.',
    'Around that victory the tradition gathered legendary embellishment, including a story that he dedicated himself to Odin in exchange for triumph. Such details are saga colouring rather than fact, and they reflect how a successful early king was remembered in heroic and pagan terms.',
    'Beyond the victory that named him, his personality is essentially inaccessible; he survives as a triumphant founder-figure of the historic Swedish monarchy rather than as a documented individual. What the tradition consistently conveys is martial success and the prestige it brought — the king who won, and was remembered for winning.'
  ],
  'olof-skotkonung': [
    'Olof Skötkonung is remembered as the first Swedish king to be baptised and to remain Christian, and that religious distinction shapes most of what tradition says about him. The sources, including Adam of Bremen and later saga material, present a ruler who accepted Christianity, minted the first Swedish coins, and is associated with the founding of a bishopric at Skara, marking him as a Christianising innovator in a still largely pagan land.',
    'The saga tradition also remembers his rivalry and eventual uneasy dealings with Olaf II of Norway, and depicts him as proud and sometimes obstinate in those disputes. His byname is variously explained, including as "tax-king" or "tribute-king," though its precise meaning is uncertain.',
    'Because the record is thin and mixes Christian approval with saga anecdote, his inner character is only faintly visible. He is remembered above all as a threshold figure — the king who made Sweden\'s monarchy Christian and began its coinage — rather than for a vividly drawn personality.'
  ],
  'anund-jacob': [
    'Anund Jacob is remembered as a Christian Swedish king who continued his father Olof Skötkonung\'s religious direction, and the sources, chiefly Adam of Bremen, treat him with approval for it. Adam praises his piety and presents him as a capable ruler who allied with Olaf II of Norway against the growing power of Cnut the Great, taking part in the Baltic campaigns against Danish-English dominance.',
    'The double name — a Norse name and the Christian baptismal name Jacob — itself reflects the religious transition of the Swedish monarchy in his time. The record emphasises his role in the northern coalition against Cnut more than any personal trait.',
    'Little of his individual temperament survives beyond Adam\'s favourable, church-minded assessment and the impression of a ruler firmly committed to the Christian and anti-Danish policies of his alliance. He is remembered as a steady continuator of his father\'s work rather than as a strongly individual figure.'
  ],
  'emund-the-old': [
    'Emund the Old is one of the more obscure Swedish kings, and his character is barely recoverable. Adam of Bremen, the main source, regarded him with suspicion, presenting him as lukewarm or unreliable in matters of the church and critical of his dealings with the archbishopric of Hamburg-Bremen — a hostile view from a writer deeply invested in that church\'s authority over the north.',
    'He is remembered for a disputed boundary settlement with Denmark and for the contested succession that followed his death, after which the old royal line gave way to a new dynasty. The negative tinge in the record reflects Adam\'s ecclesiastical priorities more than a rounded judgement of the man.',
    'The honest assessment is that Emund survives as a name at the end of a dynasty, seen mainly through the disapproval of a churchman who distrusted his independence from Hamburg-Bremen. His personality cannot be reconstructed with any confidence from so partial and unfriendly a record.'
  ],
  'stenkil': [
    'Stenkil is remembered as the founder of a new Swedish royal dynasty and as a ruler caught between Christianity and lingering paganism, a tension that defines the little we know of him. Adam of Bremen, who valued his support for the church, presents him as a friend of Christian mission, yet also records the deep resistance of pagan strongholds such as Uppsala that constrained any king of the period.',
    'A telling tradition holds that he restrained zealous Christians from destroying the great pagan temple at Uppsala, judging it too dangerous and premature — an anecdote, if accepted, suggesting a cautious and pragmatic ruler who understood the limits of his authority over a divided people.',
    'Because the sources are sparse and shaped by the church\'s interests, his personality is only dimly seen. What the record allows is the picture of a moderate, accommodating king navigating a society split between old and new religion, remembered as a careful founder rather than a forceful or vivid individual.'
  ],
  'inge-the-elder': [
    'Inge the Elder is remembered as a firmly Christian Swedish king whose faith brought him into direct conflict with the pagan party, and the saga tradition preserves a vivid story to illustrate it. According to that account, Inge refused to perform the old sacrifices at Uppsala and was driven from the throne for it, replaced by his pagan brother-in-law "Blot-Sven," before returning to kill the usurper and reclaim his kingdom for Christianity.',
    'This narrative, though shaped by later Christian storytelling, presents Inge as devout and resolute, willing to lose his crown rather than compromise his faith and determined enough to win it back. The tradition consistently casts him as a champion of the new religion against the last strength of paganism.',
    'Because the dramatic episodes come from saga sources written long after, they must be read as Christian memory rather than documented fact. But the steady direction of the tradition — a pious, stubborn king who broke with the old cult — gives Inge a clearer reputation than most early Swedish rulers, even if the details belong to legend.'
  ],
  'sverker-i-of-sweden': [
    'Sverker I of Sweden founded one of the two rival dynasties — the Sverkers and the Eriks — whose alternation defined Swedish kingship for over a century, and his memory is shaped by that long feud. The sources present a ruler who rose to unite the Swedish provinces and who favoured the church, founding or supporting monasteries such as Alvastra, but who met a violent end, murdered on his way to Christmas mass around 1156.',
    'That assassination, traditionally blamed on rivals or a servant, fixed him in memory as both a consolidator and a victim of the dynastic bloodshed he helped inaugurate. The patronage of Cistercian houses suggests genuine religious commitment, or at least a shrewd alliance with the rising power of the church.',
    'Little of his personal temperament survives beyond these documented acts — unification, monastic patronage, and a murdered death. He is remembered as the progenitor of a dynasty and an early victim of the Sverker-Erik rivalry, his individual character largely subsumed into the dynastic story.'
  ],
  'eric-ix-of-sweden': [
    'Eric IX of Sweden became Sweden\'s patron saint, and his remembered character, like Olaf of Norway\'s, is shaped decisively by that cult. The hagiographic tradition presents him as a model Christian king — just, pious, protective of the church, and a crusader who carried Christianity to the Finns — and credits him with a martyr\'s death, killed by political enemies, by tradition after hearing mass, around 1160.',
    'This portrait comes almost entirely from the legend composed to support his sainthood, written well after his death, so its specific virtues must be read as the qualities a holy king was expected to display rather than as documented biography. The historical Eric is correspondingly hard to recover beneath the saint.',
    'What the tradition consistently conveys is the image of the righteous royal martyr: devout, law-giving, and zealous in the faith. Whether the man matched the legend cannot be established, but the cult fixed his reputation firmly as Sweden\'s holy king, and that devotional memory is most of what survives of his character.'
  ],
  'knut-eriksson': [
    'Knut Eriksson, son of the sainted Eric IX, is remembered as a determined dynast who secured the Erik line\'s hold on the Swedish throne through the long feud with the Sverkers. The sources present a ruler who came to power by defeating and killing his rivals and who then held the crown for a substantial reign, consolidating royal authority and promoting the cult of his martyred father to bolster his legitimacy.',
    'His reign is associated with the strengthening of the monarchy, the building of fortifications, and cooperation with the church and the powerful magnate Birger Brosa, suggesting a capable political operator. The use of his father\'s sanctity as a dynastic asset shows a shrewd grasp of the sources of royal legitimacy.',
    'The personal man is faintly drawn, but the documented actions characterise the reign clearly enough: ruthlessness in the dynastic struggle, effective consolidation, and skilful exploitation of his father\'s holy reputation. He is remembered as a successful Erik partisan who entrenched his family\'s claim rather than as a vividly individual character.'
  ],
  'sverker-ii-of-sweden': [
    'Sverker II of Sweden, of the Sverker dynasty, is remembered through the renewed bloodshed of the Sverker-Erik feud that ultimately destroyed his kingship. The sources present a ruler raised to the throne with the support of powerful magnates and the church, who governed for a time but was then driven out by the rival Erik line and killed in battle at Gestilren in 1210 while attempting to recover his crown.',
    'His reign and fall illustrate the dependence of Swedish kings of the period on aristocratic and ecclesiastical backing, and the deadly instability of the alternating dynasties. He is associated with concessions to the church that strengthened its position in Sweden.',
    'Little of his individual character emerges from the record, which is dominated by the dynastic conflict that defined and ended his reign. He is remembered as a figure in the Sverker-Erik struggle — raised by his backers, deposed by his rivals, and killed in the attempt to return — rather than as a personality recoverable in his own right.'
  ],
  'erik-knutsson': [
    'Erik Knutsson of the Erik dynasty is remembered for decisively defeating his Sverker rival, winning the throne at the Battle of Gestilren in 1210 and bringing a measure of stability to the Swedish monarchy. The sources present him as the victor in a critical round of the dynastic war and as the first Swedish king recorded as formally crowned and anointed, a mark of the growing alignment of monarchy and church.',
    'His reign was relatively secure by the standards of the period, and his coronation signalled an effort to put royal authority on a firmer, more sacral footing. He died of illness rather than violence, an unusual fate for kings of his contested line.',
    'The personal character is thinly recorded, but the documented achievements — military victory, a stabilising reign, and the symbolic step of a church coronation — define how he is remembered. He is the Erik king who won and consolidated, his individual temperament largely hidden behind the dynastic and institutional record.'
  ],
  'birger-jarl': [
    'Birger Jarl never held the royal title himself, yet he is remembered as the most powerful and consequential figure in thirteenth-century Sweden, and the tradition credits him with the qualities of a shrewd and forceful statesman. As jarl, he ruled in practice on behalf of kings and secured the throne for his own son, founding the Folkung (Bjälbo) dynasty and exercising authority that exceeded that of the nominal monarchs.',
    'He is associated with major consolidating achievements — the strengthening of royal law, including measures protecting women and the peace of the home and church, the expansion of Swedish power into Finland, and the traditional founding of Stockholm. These point to an able administrator and lawgiver as much as a warrior.',
    'Later Swedish tradition idealised him as a founder-figure, so some of the admiration is retrospective nation-building. But the documented record supports a ruler of exceptional political skill and ambition who dominated his era without a crown — remembered for statecraft, law, and dynastic foundation rather than for personal anecdote.'
  ],
  'valdemar-of-sweden': [
    'Valdemar of Sweden, first of the Folkung dynasty to wear the crown, is remembered as a king overshadowed by his powerful father Birger Jarl and then destroyed by rivalry with his own brothers. The sources present a ruler who came to the throne as a boy under his father\'s guidance and who, after Birger\'s death, proved unable to hold his own against his ambitious siblings, losing power and eventually his freedom.',
    'A scandal involving his sister-in-law and a penitential journey damaged his reputation, and the tradition tends to present him as weak, pleasure-loving, and politically ineffective compared with the brothers who supplanted him. Such moralising should be treated with caution, as it served the interests of those who deposed him.',
    'What survives is largely the image of a king who could not master his family or his nobles, defined more by his fall than by any positive achievement. He is remembered as the ineffective first Folkung monarch, his individual qualities obscured by the partisan memory of his more successful rivals.'
  ],
  'magnus-iii-of-sweden': [
    'Magnus III of Sweden, called Ladulås ("Barn-lock"), is remembered as a capable and assertive king who seized power from his brother Valdemar and strengthened the Swedish monarchy and aristocracy alike. His byname, traditionally explained as a tribute to his protection of the peasantry against the plundering of travelling magnates, points to a reputation for imposing order and defending the common people from noble abuse.',
    'The sources present a ruler who organised the realm along more clearly feudal lines, granting privileges to a service nobility and the church and consolidating royal authority. These measures mark him as an effective institution-builder in the style of contemporary European monarchies.',
    'The personal man is faintly drawn, but the consistent memory — captured in his protective byname and his documented reforms — is of a strong, orderly king who curbed disorder and shaped the structures of the medieval Swedish state. He is remembered for effective governance rather than for vivid personal incident.'
  ],
  'birger-magnusson': [
    'Birger Magnusson of Sweden is remembered for one of the most notorious acts of treachery in Scandinavian history: the Nyköping Banquet of 1317, at which he seized and imprisoned his own brothers, the dukes Erik and Valdemar, and left them to die in captivity, reportedly of starvation. That single act of fratricidal revenge dominates his reputation.',
    'The deed grew out of years of bitter conflict among the royal brothers, and Birger\'s vengeance backfired catastrophically: the outrage provoked a rebellion that drove him from the throne and into exile, and led to the execution of his son. The sources present him as vindictive and politically disastrous, undone by the very cruelty meant to secure him.',
    'Little of his character survives apart from this dark episode, but it is enough to fix his memory firmly. He is remembered as the king whose act of treacherous revenge destroyed his own rule — a figure of dynastic tragedy whose name is bound permanently to the Nyköping Banquet.'
  ],
  'magnus-eriksson': [
    'Magnus Eriksson ruled both Sweden and Norway as a child-king and is remembered as a well-meaning but ultimately unsuccessful monarch whose long reign ended in deposition and disaster. The sources present a ruler associated with significant achievements — a national law code for Sweden, the abolition of thralldom — yet dogged by financial difficulty, noble revolt, and the catastrophe of the Black Death that struck during his reign.',
    'His reputation suffered greatly from a hostile tradition: the prophecies attributed to Saint Birgitta of Sweden condemned him sharply, and rumours about his personal conduct circulated among his enemies. This propaganda, much of it driven by aristocratic and religious opponents, shaped a harshly negative memory that modern historians treat with caution.',
    'What the record more securely supports is a ruler of reforming intentions overwhelmed by rebellion, plague, debt, and dynastic rivalry, who lost first Sweden and then his footing in Norway and drowned in exile. He is remembered as a tragic and much-maligned king, his real character partly buried beneath the condemnation of his enemies.'
  ],
  'albert-of-mecklenburg': [
    'Albert of Mecklenburg, a German prince raised to the Swedish throne by nobles seeking a pliable king, is remembered as a foreign ruler who failed to win the loyalty of his adopted realm. The sources present a monarch dependent on German favourites and resented by the Swedish aristocracy that had invited him, whose attempts to assert royal authority alienated the very magnates who had made him king.',
    'His reign collapsed in conflict with the powerful councillors and ultimately with Margaret I of Denmark, who defeated and captured him at the Battle of Falköping in 1389. A famous gibe attributed to the episode — mockery involving a whetstone and a long fool\'s cap — fixed his humiliation in tradition.',
    'The hostile Swedish memory, shaped by those who rejected him, presents a weak and unpopular foreign king out of step with his kingdom. He is remembered chiefly for his failure and capture rather than for any positive quality, a cautionary figure of misjudged foreign kingship.'
  ],
  'charles-viii-of-sweden': [
    'Charles VIII of Sweden lived one of the most turbulent royal careers of the late medieval north, remembered as an ambitious and resilient magnate who seized and lost the Swedish throne repeatedly amid the strains of the Kalmar Union. The sources present a powerful noble who maneuvered his way to kingship as a champion of Swedish independence against Danish union rule, only to be driven into exile and to return more than once.',
    'His thrice-held, thrice-lost crown reflects both his persistence and the impossible volatility of Swedish politics in the union era, torn between nationalist and unionist factions and a fractious aristocracy. He also briefly held Norway, extending the drama of his career across the union realms.',
    'The tradition presents him as ambitious, tenacious, and adept at exploiting anti-Danish sentiment, though never able to secure lasting stability. He is remembered as the embattled symbol of Swedish separatism in his age — a determined survivor whose repeated returns testify to ambition and resilience more than to any settled achievement.'
  ]
})

Object.assign(content, {
  'charles-martel': [
    'Charles Martel — "the Hammer" — is remembered through a byname that captures his reputation for relentless martial force. The Frankish sources, especially the continuators of the chronicle of Fredegar who wrote under his descendants, present him as a tireless and victorious commander who seized power as mayor of the palace, crushed his rivals, and turned back an Arab-Berber army at the Battle of Tours in 732, an action later Christian tradition magnified into the salvation of Europe.',
    'These sources were sympathetic to the Carolingian house he founded, so the heroic colouring is partisan. What the record supports is a ruler of hard ambition and military single-mindedness who fought almost ceaselessly, reorganised Frankish forces, and did not hesitate to confiscate church lands to reward his warriors — an act that earned him criticism from churchmen even as he protected Christian Francia.',
    'The consistent portrait is of a forceful, pragmatic war-leader more than a cultivated statesman: a man who built power through victory and the rewarding of followers. The epithet that defined him in memory — the Hammer — reflects how thoroughly his contemporaries and heirs understood him through the language of decisive force.'
  ],
  'abd-al-rahman-al-ghafiqi': [
    'Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi is known almost entirely through the campaign that ended his life, and his personality survives mainly in the way Christian and Muslim sources frame the commander who fell at the Battle of Tours in 732. Arabic tradition remembers him as a capable and pious governor of al-Andalus who led a major raid deep into Frankish Gaul, while Latin sources cast him as the leader of the great heathen host turned back by Charles Martel.',
    'Because he is preserved chiefly as an adversary in others\' narratives, his individual character is largely inaccessible. What can be inferred from his documented actions is the boldness of a commander willing to drive far north of the Pyrenees in a major expedition, and the misfortune of meeting a disciplined Frankish army that destroyed his force and killed him.',
    'The honest assessment is that he is remembered for the consequence of his defeat rather than for any rounded portrait. He stands in the record as the commander of Tours — bold in his advance, fatal in his miscalculation — his real personality lost behind the symbolic weight later writers attached to the battle.'
  ],
  'harold-godwinson': [
    'Harold Godwinson is remembered through the lens of the Norman Conquest, and his character is contested precisely because the victors needed to justify his overthrow. Norman sources, including the Bayeux Tapestry and William of Poitiers, painted him as an oath-breaker and usurper to legitimise the invasion, while English and later sympathetic tradition saw a capable, brave, and rightful king cut down at the Battle of Hastings.',
    'What the record more securely supports is an able and energetic leader: the most powerful earl in England before his accession, a successful commander against the Welsh, and a king who, within weeks of taking the throne, marched north to destroy Harald Hardrada at the Battle of Stamford Bridge before turning to face the Normans in the south. That double campaign shows decisiveness and stamina under extreme pressure.',
    'His personality is hard to separate from propaganda, but the documented actions point to a vigorous, courageous ruler undone by the impossible timing of two invasions. He is remembered as the last crowned Anglo-Saxon king — brave and competent in the English memory, perjured and doomed in the Norman — his real character framed by the conquest that destroyed him.'
  ],
  'henry-v-of-england': [
    'Henry V of England is remembered as the model of the disciplined warrior-king, and contemporary and near-contemporary sources present a ruler of formidable seriousness, piety, and command. After a reportedly wild youth, he is said to have transformed on his accession into a grave, devout, and rigorously self-controlled monarch — a reformation of character that chroniclers emphasised and that Shakespeare later made famous.',
    'His personality expressed itself in relentless discipline and religious conviction: he enforced strict order in his armies, attributed his stunning victory at the Battle of Agincourt to God, and pursued the conquest of France with cold, methodical determination, including the harsh treatment of besieged towns. Admirers saw justice and resolve; the same qualities could appear as severity, as in the killing of prisoners at Agincourt and the unbending prosecution of his claims.',
    'The portrait is shaped partly by patriotic memory of England\'s greatest medieval military triumph, but it is well grounded: a pious, austere, intensely focused ruler whose self-mastery and martial competence made him formidable. He is remembered as the embodiment of righteous, disciplined kingship — admirable and hard in equal measure.'
  ],
  'charles-d-albret': [
    'Charles d\'Albret, Constable of France, is remembered mainly through the catastrophe of the Battle of Agincourt, where he commanded and died, and his character survives largely as part of the post-mortem on that defeat. As Constable he held nominal command of a French army riven by aristocratic rivalry and indiscipline, and the sources suggest a leader unable to impose order on the proud, fractious nobles who would not subordinate themselves to his authority.',
    'Whether the disaster reflects personal failure or the impossible task of controlling a divided command is hard to judge; the chroniclers, looking for explanations, tend to distribute blame across the French leadership rather than fix a clear portrait of d\'Albret himself. He died in the slaughter of the French nobility that day, sharing the fate of the men he could not master.',
    'The honest assessment is that his individual character is faintly recorded, overshadowed by the magnitude of Agincourt. He is remembered as the commander of a doomed and ungovernable army — a figure of the structural weakness of French chivalry in 1415 more than a vividly drawn personality.'
  ],
  'constantine-xi-palaiologos': [
    'Constantine XI Palaiologos, the last Byzantine emperor, is remembered through the heroism of his death, and that final stand shapes the whole tradition of his character. Greek sources and later memory present a ruler of dignity and courage who, facing the overwhelming Ottoman siege of 1453, refused offers to flee or surrender and chose to die defending Constantinople, reportedly throwing off his imperial insignia to fall fighting as an ordinary soldier in the breach.',
    'That image — the emperor dying with his city — fixed him in Greek tradition as a tragic and noble figure, and gave rise to the legend of the "marble emperor" who would one day return. The sources emphasise resolve, piety, and the acceptance of a doomed duty rather than military brilliance, since the situation he inherited was beyond saving.',
    'Because much of the portrait is shaped by the pathos of the fall and by later national mourning, it is more elegiac than analytical. But the central, well-attested fact — his refusal to abandon Constantinople and his death in its defence — supports the memory of a steadfast and courageous ruler who met an impossible end with honour.'
  ],
  'romanos-iv-diogenes': [
    'Romanos IV Diogenes is remembered through the disaster of the Battle of Manzikert in 1071 and the betrayal that surrounded it, and his character is drawn largely from that tragedy. The Byzantine sources, notably the historian Michael Attaleiates who knew him, present a brave and energetic soldier-emperor who tried to restore the army and confront the Seljuk threat, but who was undermined by the entrenched court aristocracy that resented him as a military outsider.',
    'His defeat and capture at Manzikert owed much to the treachery of subordinates who failed or abandoned him, and the aftermath was crueller still: deposed by the rival Doukas faction, he was blinded and left to die of his wounds. The sources, even those critical of his judgement, convey sympathy for a courageous ruler destroyed as much by internal enemies as by the Seljuks.',
    'The consistent portrait is of a vigorous, well-meaning, and personally brave emperor overwhelmed by the factionalism of the Byzantine court. He is remembered as a tragic figure — a capable soldier undone by betrayal — whose fall came to symbolise the deeper political sickness that the catastrophe at Manzikert exposed.'
  ],
  'alp-arslan': [
    'Alp Arslan, the Seljuk sultan whose name means "Heroic Lion," is remembered in the Islamic and Byzantine traditions as a formidable warrior and, in a famous episode, as a ruler capable of striking magnanimity. Persian and Arabic sources, shaped by admiration and by the influence of his great vizier Nizam al-Mulk, present a commanding, energetic conqueror who expanded Seljuk power and won the decisive victory over the Byzantines at the Battle of Manzikert.',
    'The most celebrated story of his character concerns the treatment of the captured emperor Romanos IV: tradition holds that Alp Arslan received his beaten enemy with courtesy, set his foot symbolically on the emperor\'s neck and then raised him up, negotiated terms, and released him — an anecdote of chivalric restraint that became central to his legend. Such stories are shaped by literary convention and should be read as tradition rather than verbatim fact.',
    'What the record consistently conveys is a powerful and capable sultan, admired for both martial vigour and, in the tradition, a measure of generosity toward a defeated foe. He is remembered as the heroic conqueror of Manzikert, his reputation balanced between fearsome military success and the famous magnanimity the sources chose to attach to it.'
  ],
  'tostig-godwinson': [
    'Tostig Godwinson is remembered as the embittered brother whose grievance helped bring on the crises of 1066, and the sources present a proud and increasingly desperate figure consumed by rivalry with his brother Harold Godwinson. As earl of Northumbria he provoked a revolt by his harsh rule, and when Harold failed to restore him, Tostig turned against his own family, allying with foreign enemies of England.',
    'That alliance led him to join Harald Hardrada\'s invasion, and he died alongside the Norwegian king at the Battle of Stamford Bridge — killed in a cause aimed squarely at his brother\'s throne. The sources, English in origin, present his career as a study in wounded pride curdling into treachery, though they also acknowledge the real grievance of his loss of office.',
    'His personality survives as a portrait of resentment and ambition: a man who would rather see England invaded than accept his own fall. He is remembered as the brother whose bitterness opened a door to disaster, a figure of dynastic tragedy whose defection sharpened the chaos of the year of three battles.'
  ],
  'philip-ii-of-france': [
    'Philip II of France, called Augustus, is remembered as a cold, patient, and supremely calculating ruler who built the power of the French crown through administration and guile rather than personal glory. Contemporary chroniclers, including the partisan royal biographer Rigord, present a king of shrewd political intelligence who expanded the royal domain enormously — above all by stripping King John of Normandy and most of the Angevin lands in France through legal maneuvering and war.',
    'He was no romantic warrior in the mould of his rival Richard the Lionheart, with whom he quarreled on the Third Crusade before returning home early to pursue his real interest: the methodical aggrandisement of his kingdom. His victory at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214 secured his gains, but his characteristic instruments were the bailli, the account-book, and the patient exploitation of feudal law.',
    'The sources present a ruler who could be devious, ungenerous, and harsh — his treatment of the Jews and his tangled marital politics drew criticism — but undeniably effective. He is remembered as the architect of the medieval French state: unglamorous, distrustful, and relentlessly competent, a king who won by calculation what others sought in battle.'
  ],
  'otto-iv': [
    'Otto IV is remembered as the Welf emperor whose promising career collapsed into failure, and his character is drawn largely from the arc of that downfall. Raised partly at the Angevin court of his uncle Richard the Lionheart, he won the German throne in a long civil war against the Hohenstaufen, but the sources present a ruler whose ambition outran his political judgement, particularly when he provoked the papacy by invading territories Pope Innocent III regarded as his own.',
    'That breach led to his excommunication and to the rise of his Hohenstaufen rival Frederick II, and his fate was sealed at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214, where his defeat alongside his English allies effectively ended his imperial authority. He spent his last years sidelined and powerless, a discarded emperor.',
    'The hostile clerical sources, written after his break with the church, emphasise overreach and broken promises, so the portrait is unsympathetic. What the record supports is an ambitious ruler whose miscalculation — antagonising the pope who had supported him — destroyed a reign that had begun with real advantages. He is remembered as the emperor undone at Bouvines.'
  ],
  'ferdinand-of-flanders': [
    'Ferdinand of Flanders is remembered chiefly through his rebellion against Philip II of France and the long captivity that followed, and his character survives as part of that drama of pride and downfall. A Portuguese prince who became count of Flanders by marriage, he resented Philip\'s heavy-handed treatment — including the seizure of disputed towns — and joined the great coalition against him, fighting at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214.',
    'His defeat and capture there made him a symbol of humbled rebellion: he was paraded and imprisoned for many years, his very name (Ferrand) the subject of grim French wordplay about being "shod with iron." The sources present a proud vassal who gambled on overthrowing his overlord and paid with more than a decade of confinement.',
    'Little of his inner character emerges beyond the impression of wounded pride and ambition that drove him to revolt. He is remembered as one of the defeated princes of Bouvines — the rebellious count whose long imprisonment underscored the completeness of Philip II\'s triumph — rather than as a rounded personality in his own right.'
  ],
  'frederick-i-barbarossa': [
    'Frederick I Barbarossa is remembered as the embodiment of imperial majesty and stubborn ambition, a ruler whose grand vision of restored Roman authority drove decades of conflict. Contemporary sources, including the admiring account by his uncle Otto of Freising, present a commanding, dignified, and energetic emperor of striking presence — the red beard that gave him his Italian byname became part of his legend — who pursued the dignity of the empire with relentless determination.',
    'His personality expressed itself in pride, persistence, and a fierce insistence on imperial rights, which brought him into long wars with the Lombard cities and the papacy. His humbling at the Battle of Legnano in 1176 and his dramatic reconciliation with the pope showed a ruler who could, when forced, bend without breaking. Chivalric and chronicle tradition admired his sense of honour and his eventual death on crusade, drowning in the Saleph River.',
    'Because much of the portrait comes from imperial partisans, the grandeur is partly cultivated, and German legend later turned him into a sleeping hero who would one day return. But the consistent core — proud, tenacious, conscious of majesty, and unyielding in pursuit of imperial authority — is well grounded in the long, combative record of his reign.'
  ],
  'guido-da-landriano': [
    'Guido da Landriano is among the most obscure figures in the archive, a Milanese commander remembered for his association with the Lombard League\'s resistance to Frederick I Barbarossa rather than for any documented personal character. The sources preserve him as a military figure of the communal armies that confronted the emperor, but they offer little beyond his role in that struggle.',
    'What can honestly be said is shaped by context rather than individual report: he belonged to the civic-military leadership of the north Italian cities that organised, against the odds, to defend their independence against imperial power, culminating in the league\'s victory at the Battle of Legnano in 1176. Commanders of this kind drew their authority from the communes they served rather than from dynastic right.',
    'The honest assessment is that his personality cannot be recovered from the surviving record. He is remembered as a representative of the Lombard communal resistance — a name attached to the military effort of the cities — rather than as an individual whose temperament the sources allow us to describe.'
  ]
})

Object.assign(content, {
  'alfonso-viii-of-castile': [
    'Alfonso VIII of Castile is remembered as a determined and resilient warrior-king whose reputation was forged by both a great defeat and a great victory. The Christian sources present a ruler of personal courage and persistence who suffered a serious reverse against the Almohads at Alarcos in 1195 — a humiliation he is said to have felt deeply — and then spent years assembling the coalition that won the decisive Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212.',
    'That comeback defined his memory: a king who turned disaster into the greatest Christian triumph of the Iberian Reconquista, securing crusading support and uniting rival kingdoms in a common effort. The sources emphasise his resolve, his piety in framing the campaign as holy war, and his capacity to learn from defeat.',
    'He was also a patron of learning, credited with founding an early Castilian centre of higher study. The consistent portrait is of a tenacious and pious ruler, marked by the contrast between Alarcos and Las Navas, remembered above all for the persistence that carried him from defeat to the victory that reshaped the peninsula.'
  ],
  'muhammad-al-nasir': [
    'Muhammad al-Nasir, the Almohad caliph, is remembered chiefly through the catastrophic defeat at the Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212, and his character survives mostly in the shadow of that disaster. The sources — both the triumphant Christian accounts and the more critical Muslim ones — present a ruler whose reign marked the turning point of Almohad decline, and who failed to retrieve a battle that broke his army.',
    'Arabic tradition tended to judge him harshly in hindsight, contrasting the scale of the defeat with the resources he commanded and questioning his leadership in the field. Christian sources, naturally, magnified the providential nature of their victory over him. Between these partisan extremes, the individual man is hard to recover.',
    'What the record supports is a caliph who presided over a decisive reversal in Almohad fortunes and could not master the crisis it created, dying not long after. He is remembered as the defeated commander of Las Navas — a ruler associated with the collapse of Almohad power in Iberia rather than with any clearly drawn personal qualities.'
  ],
  'robert-the-bruce': [
    'Robert the Bruce is remembered as the patient, resilient architect of Scottish independence, and his character carries the drama of a man who began as a compromised, oath-shifting noble and became a national hero. Scottish tradition — most influentially the epic poem by John Barbour — emphasised courage, perseverance, and inspirational leadership, while the early record also preserves the more troubling start of his bid for the crown: the killing of his rival John Comyn in a church, an act of sacrilege that brought excommunication.',
    'The years that followed, in which Bruce was a hunted fugitive who rebuilt his cause from near-nothing, fixed his reputation for dogged resilience — the much-loved later legend of the persevering spider belongs to this image. His generalship in guerrilla warfare and his decisive victory at the Battle of Bannockburn in 1314 showed a shrewd, adaptable commander who avoided pitched battle until the odds favoured him.',
    'The heroic portrait is partly the work of patriotic memory, and the early Bruce was a calculating and sometimes ruthless dynast. But the documented arc — from the murder at Dumfries through years of defeat to triumph and the securing of Scottish independence — supports the core of the legend: a determined, politically astute, and resilient leader who grew into the role of liberator-king.'
  ],
  'edward-ii-of-england': [
    'Edward II of England is remembered as one of medieval England\'s most disastrous kings, and the sources are nearly unanimous in presenting a ruler whose personal favouritism and political ineptitude destroyed him. Chroniclers, largely hostile, dwell on his devotion to favourites — first Piers Gaveston, later the Despensers — whose elevation enraged the baronage and drained royal authority, and on his lack of interest in the martial kingship his barons expected.',
    'They also record his humiliating defeat by Robert the Bruce at the Battle of Bannockburn, his inability to manage the great magnates, and the descent of his reign into civil conflict, ending in his deposition and murder. The sources note, sometimes with disapproval, his fondness for rustic pastimes unbecoming a king, and his emotional attachment to his favourites above his nobles and queen.',
    'The portrait is shaped by the contempt of chroniclers writing after his fall, so the personal failings are emphasised to explain the catastrophe. What the record consistently conveys is a ruler who inspired loyalty in a narrow circle and alienation in almost everyone else — remembered as devoted, stubborn, and politically calamitous, a king undone by misjudged affections and incapacity to rule.'
  ],
  'edward-iii-of-england': [
    'Edward III of England is remembered as the very model of the successful chivalric king, and contemporary sources present a charismatic, martial, and politically shrewd ruler who restored the prestige of the English crown after his father\'s disasters. Froissart and others celebrated his cultivation of knightly culture — the founding of the Order of the Garter, the splendour of his court — alongside the stunning victories of the early Hundred Years\' War at Crécy and beyond.',
    'His personality, as the sources draw it, combined genuine military gifts with a calculated mastery of the theatre of kingship: he understood that magnificence, generosity, and shared glory bound the aristocracy to him, turning the energies that had destroyed Edward II to his advantage. He was ambitious and acquisitive of honour, pressing his claim to the French throne with relentless confidence.',
    'The admiring portrait owes something to the chivalric enthusiasm of his chroniclers and to the contrast with his father and his troubled last years, when age and the loss of his heir clouded the reign. But the core is well grounded: a charismatic, war-loving, and politically astute king who made the English monarchy glamorous and feared, remembered as the architect of its fourteenth-century greatness.'
  ],
  'edward-the-black-prince': [
    'Edward, the Black Prince, is remembered as the supreme chivalric warrior of his age, and the chronicle tradition — above all Froissart — celebrated him as the very flower of knighthood: brave, generous to noble foes, and brilliantly successful in the field at Crécy and at his great victory at the Battle of Poitiers, where he captured the French king John II and famously waited on his royal prisoner at table.',
    'That courteous treatment of a defeated king became a centerpiece of his chivalric legend. Yet the same prince was responsible for the brutal sack of Limoges, where the slaughter of inhabitants showed a harder, more ruthless face that the chivalric sources struggled to reconcile with their hero. Modern assessment holds both together: gallantry toward noble equals coexisting with severity toward those outside that charmed circle.',
    'His later years were marked by illness that ended his campaigning and preceded his death before he could inherit the throne. The portrait is shaped by the chivalric admiration of his chroniclers, but the documented record supports its core — a gifted, courageous, and magnificent commander whose chivalry was real but selective, remembered as the ideal knight who never became king.'
  ],
  'john-ii-of-france': [
    'John II of France, called the Good, carries a byname that has puzzled posterity, since his reign was among the most disastrous in French history. The sources suggest the epithet meant "valiant" or "brave" rather than virtuous, and his character does show a certain reckless courage — most famously at the Battle of Poitiers in 1356, where he fought on after the battle was lost and was captured, reportedly refusing to flee.',
    'That bravery sat beside poor judgement and a temper for harsh, impulsive justice: he executed the Constable of France on suspicion of treason early in his reign and proved unable to manage the factions and financial crises that the war and the Black Death had inflamed. His long captivity in England, and his celebrated decision to return voluntarily into custody when a hostage broke parole, struck contemporaries as a point of chivalric honour.',
    'The portrait is mixed and somewhat paradoxical: personally brave and bound by aristocratic notions of honour, yet politically maladroit and presiding over national humiliation. He is remembered as a king whose chivalric instincts — courage in defeat, fidelity to his word — could not compensate for the disasters of his rule.'
  ],
  'prince-lazar': [
    'Prince Lazar of Serbia is remembered through the prism of the Battle of Kosovo in 1389 and the vast tradition of epic poetry and Orthodox veneration that grew from it, which makes the historical man hard to separate from the martyr-hero. Serbian sources and later folk epic present him as a noble and pious ruler who chose a "heavenly kingdom" over an earthly one, accepting death in battle against the Ottomans rather than submission — a choice framed as conscious Christian martyrdom.',
    'This portrait is shaped overwhelmingly by the cult that followed his death: the Serbian Orthodox Church canonised him, and the Kosovo epics turned him into the symbolic centre of Serbian national and religious identity. The qualities attributed to him — piety, honour, self-sacrifice — are those of a sacred hero as much as a documented individual.',
    'The historical Lazar was a capable regional prince who united much of the fragmented Serbian lands and led the coalition that met the Ottomans at Kosovo, where he was captured and executed. But his remembered character belongs as much to legend and devotion as to record, and the honest approach is to present the martyr-hero of tradition while noting that it is tradition that gives him his vivid moral shape.'
  ],
  'murad-i': [
    'Murad I is remembered as one of the architects of Ottoman greatness, a sultan who combined relentless conquest with the building of durable institutions. The sources, mainly later Ottoman tradition, present a capable and energetic ruler who expanded the emerging state deep into the Balkans and Anatolia, organised its military and administrative structures, and was respected as a serious and formidable leader.',
    'His death gave his memory its most dramatic feature: he was killed at the Battle of Kosovo in 1389, by tradition assassinated by a Serbian noble who gained access to him after the fighting — a striking end for a victorious commander, and one that Ottoman and Serbian traditions remember very differently. The episode fixed him as a martyr-conqueror in Ottoman memory.',
    'Because the fullest accounts are later and celebratory, the portrait is shaped by the needs of a dynasty looking back on its founders. What the record consistently supports is a determined and institutionally minded ruler whose conquests and reforms were central to the Ottoman rise — remembered as a powerful empire-builder whose dramatic death at Kosovo sealed his legend.'
  ],
  'wladyslaw-ii-jagiello': [
    'Władysław II Jagiełło is remembered as the Lithuanian grand duke who became a Polish king and a Christian convert, and the sources — Polish, and notably the chronicler Jan Długosz — present a complex figure not always to their taste. He came to the Polish throne through marriage and through his acceptance of Christianity, uniting Poland and Lithuania, and Długosz, while acknowledging his achievements, often portrayed him as cautious, reserved, and shaped by his pagan Lithuanian origins.',
    'His most celebrated moment was the great victory over the Teutonic Order at the Battle of Grunwald in 1410, where the sources credit him with patient generalship — notably his deliberate, prayerful slowness in committing to battle, which his critics read as hesitation and his admirers as prudence. That ambiguity runs through his portrayal: a careful, devout, and politically shrewd ruler whose restraint could look like either wisdom or timidity.',
    'Because the main source viewed him through a Polish and clerical lens not wholly sympathetic to his Lithuanian background, the portrait carries a faint condescension that modern historians discount. What emerges is a long-reigning, patient, and consequential ruler — founder of a dynasty and of the Polish-Lithuanian union — remembered for the deliberate, unflashy statecraft that won Grunwald and bound two realms together.'
  ],
  'vytautas': [
    'Vytautas the Great is remembered as the towering figure of medieval Lithuanian history, and the tradition presents a ruler of immense energy, ambition, and political cunning. Lithuanian, Polish, and even hostile Teutonic sources convey a restless and formidable leader who maneuvered through shifting alliances — with the Teutonic Order, with his cousin Jagiełło, with Rus\' and Tatar powers — always in pursuit of Lithuanian power and his own authority as grand duke.',
    'His reign saw Lithuania reach its greatest territorial extent, stretching toward the Black Sea, and his leadership was central to the victory over the Teutonic Order at the Battle of Grunwald in 1410. The sources emphasise his decisiveness, his capacity for bold reversals of policy, and his nearly successful drive to obtain a royal crown of his own, frustrated only by his death.',
    'The portrait is partly shaped by later Lithuanian national pride, which made him a founding hero, but the documented record supports the core: a dynamic, calculating, and supremely ambitious ruler who dominated the politics of the eastern Baltic for decades. He is remembered as Lithuania\'s greatest medieval ruler — relentless, adaptable, and never satisfied with the power he held.'
  ],
  'ulrich-von-jungingen': [
    'Ulrich von Jungingen, Grand Master of the Teutonic Order, is remembered through the catastrophe he led the order into at the Battle of Grunwald in 1410, and his character is drawn largely from the sources\' search for an explanation of that defeat. He is presented, especially in the Polish tradition of Jan Długosz, as proud, aggressive, and overconfident — a leader whose haughtiness was symbolised by the famous story of his sending two naked swords to his enemies before the battle as an arrogant taunt.',
    'That gesture, whether historical or legendary, captured how his adversaries chose to remember him: as the embodiment of crusading pride brought low. He died fighting on the field, and the destruction of the order\'s army that day marked the beginning of the Teutonic Knights\' long decline.',
    'Because the fullest portrait comes from the victors, the emphasis on arrogance and rashness is partisan, framing his defeat as the just punishment of overweening pride. What the record supports is an aggressive and confident leader who sought the decisive battle and lost it disastrously — remembered, above all, as the Grand Master who fell at Grunwald.'
  ],
  'philip-vi-of-france': [
    'Philip VI of France, first of the Valois kings, is remembered as a ruler whose reign opened with promise and collapsed into disaster, and the sources present a king more comfortable with the trappings of chivalry than with the hard realities that overwhelmed him. He came to a contested throne and initially cut an impressive figure, but the chroniclers record a monarch outmatched by the crises of the early Hundred Years\' War.',
    'His defeat by Edward III at the Battle of Crécy in 1346 — where French chivalric impetuosity shattered against English discipline — and the loss of Calais, followed by the arrival of the Black Death, fixed his reign in memory as a sequence of catastrophes. The sources suggest a ruler of conventional aristocratic instincts who lacked the strategic grasp to counter a more innovative enemy.',
    'The portrait is shaped by hindsight and by the contrast with his able opponent, so it leans toward blame. What the record supports is a king of ordinary chivalric outlook caught in extraordinary times, unable to master the military, financial, and epidemic disasters of his reign. He is remembered as the unlucky founder of the Valois line, presiding over the opening defeats of a long war.'
  ],
  'edward-i-of-england': [
    'Edward I of England is remembered as a hard, imposing, and relentless king — "the Hammer of the Scots," as a later inscription on his tomb proclaimed — and the sources present a ruler of formidable will, physical stature, and legal-minded ambition. Contemporaries respected and feared him: a lawgiver who reshaped English statute and administration, a crusader in his youth, and a conqueror who subdued Wales and pursued the subjugation of Scotland with grim determination.',
    'His personality combined genuine concern for law and royal rights with a capacity for harshness that the sources do not conceal — the brutal execution of William Wallace, the heavy hand in Scotland and Wales, and the expulsion of the Jews from England in 1290. He could be vengeful and unyielding once opposed, and his temper was remembered as fearsome.',
    'The portrait is broadly consistent across admiring and critical sources: a powerful, intelligent, and ruthless ruler who strengthened the English state and crushed resistance without mercy. He is remembered as one of England\'s most effective and most severe medieval kings — a builder of law and a breaker of enemies in equal measure.'
  ],
  'john-comyn': [
    'John Comyn, the Scottish noble killed by Robert the Bruce, is remembered largely through the lens of that violent death and the partisan traditions that followed it. As one of the leading claimants and patriots in the Scottish cause, he was a powerful magnate and rival to Bruce, and the pro-Bruce tradition that came to dominate Scottish memory tended to cast him unfavourably to justify his killing in the church at Dumfries in 1306.',
    'The hostile framing makes his character hard to assess fairly: he was a serious political figure with his own strong claim and following, not the mere obstacle that later Bruce-centred narrative implied. His murder — sacrilegious and politically explosive — was as much a sign of Bruce\'s ruthlessness as of any failing in Comyn.',
    'What the record allows is the picture of a major Scottish noble and rival whose reputation was shaped by the man who killed him and then triumphed. He is remembered chiefly as the victim of Bruce\'s decisive and bloody seizure of leadership — a figure whose own qualities are obscured by the partisan memory of his enemies.'
  ],
  'edward-the-confessor': [
    'Edward the Confessor is remembered through a haze of sanctity, and his character is shaped decisively by the cult that canonised him as a holy king. The hagiographic tradition, developed after his death, presented him as pious, gentle, chaste, and otherworldly — a king more drawn to religion and the building of Westminster Abbey than to the hard business of rule, credited with healing miracles and saintly visions.',
    'This devotional portrait served the interests of those who promoted his cult, and it tends to soften the political realities of his reign, in which he maneuvered among powerful earls, especially the Godwin family, with more guile than the saintly image suggests. The childlessness that the tradition framed as holy chastity also produced the succession crisis that led to the Norman Conquest.',
    'The honest assessment holds the saint and the politician together: a genuinely pious king, but also a ruler who navigated a difficult reign and whose disputed succession changed history. His remembered character is largely the creation of the cult of the Confessor — gentle and holy in tradition — while the working king beneath that image is harder to recover.'
  ]
})

Object.assign(content, {
  'pope-urban-ii': [
    'Pope Urban II is remembered as a figure of commanding eloquence and political vision, the pope whose sermon at Clermont in 1095 launched the First Crusade. The sources — several rival accounts of his speech, all written after the crusade\'s success — present a persuasive and ambitious churchman who fused the cause of eastern Christians, the liberation of Jerusalem, and the reform of a violent knightly class into a single galvanising appeal.',
    'Because the famous speech survives only in later, divergent reconstructions, his exact words and intentions are uncertain, and the sources tell us as much about how the crusade was later understood as about Urban himself. What the record supports is a skilled administrator and reformer, shaped by the Cluniac monastic tradition and by the struggle to assert papal authority, who grasped the mobilising power of penitential warfare.',
    'He did not live to learn of Jerusalem\'s capture, dying days after the city fell without the news reaching him. He is remembered as the visionary instigator whose preaching unleashed forces far beyond his control — an eloquent, ambitious reformer whose single sermon reshaped the medieval world.'
  ],
  'peter-the-hermit': [
    'Peter the Hermit is remembered as a charismatic popular preacher whose fervour helped ignite the chaotic People\'s Crusade, and the sources present a figure of intense, almost theatrical religious magnetism. Contemporary chroniclers describe a small, unkempt, barefoot ascetic riding a donkey, whose passionate preaching drew enormous crowds of common people into a disorganised and ultimately disastrous expedition ahead of the main crusade.',
    'The tradition is ambivalent: admiring of his power to move the masses, yet aware that the movement he inspired collapsed into violence — including pogroms against Jewish communities — and was destroyed by the Turks before reaching Jerusalem. Some later legend exaggerated his role, even crediting him with originating the whole crusade, which the sources do not securely support.',
    'What the record consistently conveys is a man of extraordinary popular charisma and religious intensity, better at kindling enthusiasm than at directing it. He is remembered as the firebrand preacher of the People\'s Crusade — a vivid embodiment of the volatile popular piety that the crusade movement unleashed and could not control.'
  ],
  'adhemar-of-le-puy': [
    'Adhemar of Le Puy, the papal legate of the First Crusade, is remembered as its steadying moral and spiritual authority, and the sources present a figure of unusual calm, prudence, and unifying influence among a quarrelsome leadership. As the pope\'s representative he combined religious standing with practical sense, and the chroniclers credit him with holding the fractious crusader princes together and maintaining discipline and morale through the ordeal of the campaign.',
    'He was also remembered as personally courageous, taking part in the fighting, and as a calming presence in crises such as the desperate siege of Antioch, where he helped manage the controversy over the Holy Lance. His death from plague at Antioch was felt as a serious blow, removing the one leader whose authority the others broadly respected.',
    'The portrait, drawn by chroniclers who valued the unity he provided, is consistently favourable. What it conveys is a wise, moderate, and respected leader — pious without fanaticism, brave without rivalry for personal power — remembered as the indispensable unifying conscience of the First Crusade, whose loss the expedition keenly felt.'
  ],
  'godfrey-of-bouillon': [
    'Godfrey of Bouillon became the central hero of crusading legend, and his remembered character is shaped as much by that later idealisation as by the record. Chosen as the first Latin ruler of Jerusalem after the city\'s capture in 1099, he famously declined the title of king, reportedly unwilling to wear a crown of gold where Christ had worn one of thorns — a gesture of piety that became the cornerstone of his saintly reputation.',
    'Later tradition made him one of the Nine Worthies, the model crusader-knight, pious and valiant, and this idealisation must be weighed against a more ordinary historical figure: a capable but not preeminent noble among the crusade\'s leaders, elevated partly because he was less divisive than rivals like Raymond IV of Toulouse. The famous renunciation of the crown may owe as much to political circumstance as to humility.',
    'What the record supports is a genuinely devout and respected leader, brave in the field and acceptable to his peers, who died within a year of his elevation. The luminous legend — the perfect Christian knight — belongs largely to later memory, and an honest portrait keeps the pious, competent nobleman distinct from the idealised hero he became.'
  ],
  'baldwin-i-of-jerusalem': [
    'Baldwin I of Jerusalem is remembered as the hard, ambitious, and effective founder of the crusader kingdom\'s monarchy, a striking contrast to his sainted brother Godfrey of Bouillon. The sources present a worldly and calculating leader who, unlike Godfrey, had no hesitation about taking the title of king, and who built the Kingdom of Jerusalem into a functioning state through relentless campaigning, territorial expansion, and political shrewdness.',
    'His career showed both daring and opportunism: he had earlier broken away from the main crusade to seize Edessa for himself, demonstrating the personal ambition that the chroniclers note. As king he was energetic, pragmatic, and willing to use any advantage, including marriages of convenience that scandalised some churchmen, to strengthen his position.',
    'The portrait is of a ruler defined by competence and ambition rather than piety — the practical state-builder beside Godfrey\'s holy reputation. He is remembered as the true architect of the crusader monarchy: a calculating, capable, and determined king who turned a precarious conquest into a durable kingdom.'
  ],
  'baldwin-ii-of-jerusalem': [
    'Baldwin II of Jerusalem is remembered as a pious, capable, and resilient ruler who consolidated the crusader kingdom through a reign marked by both achievement and adversity. The sources present a leader of genuine religious devotion — noted for the calluses on his knees from prayer — combined with the toughness of a frontier warrior who spent much of his reign campaigning and even endured a period of captivity at Muslim hands.',
    'His reign saw the kingdom strengthened and the military orders, especially the Knights Templar, take shape under his patronage, and he managed the succession carefully by marrying his daughter Melisende to a powerful western noble. The chroniclers credit him with prudence and steadiness through repeated crises, including his capture and ransom.',
    'The portrait is broadly favourable, presenting a ruler who balanced piety with practical statecraft and held the kingdom together through difficult years. He is remembered as a devout and durable king — less glamorous than the founding heroes but essential to the survival and institutional development of the crusader state.'
  ],
  'raymond-iv-of-toulouse': [
    'Raymond IV of Toulouse is remembered as the proud, wealthy, and difficult elder statesman of the First Crusade, a leader whose ambition and prickliness repeatedly set him at odds with his fellow princes. The sources present the oldest and richest of the crusade\'s commanders, deeply pious and close to the papal legate Adhemar, yet so conscious of his own status that his rivalries — especially with Bohemond of Antioch — hampered the leadership at crucial moments.',
    'He was associated with the controversy over the Holy Lance at Antioch, which his own followers had discovered and which his rivals doubted, and his ambitions for a princely territory of his own were frustrated more than once. The chroniclers present him as devout and serious but also stubborn, jealous of precedence, and not always able to subordinate his pride to the common cause.',
    'What the record supports is a powerful, religiously committed, but contentious leader whose age and wealth gave him standing he could not always convert into authority. He is remembered as the grand, quarrelsome senior of the crusade — pious and formidable, yet undone in his ambitions by the rivalries his own pride helped sustain.'
  ],
  'bohemond-i-of-antioch': [
    'Bohemond of Antioch is remembered as the most cunning and self-interested of the First Crusade\'s leaders, a brilliant and ruthless Norman adventurer whose ambition was never far from the surface. The sources — including the admiring yet wary Gesta Francorum and the hostile but penetrating account of the Byzantine princess Anna Komnene — present a tall, charismatic, and formidable warrior of restless ambition, whose real goal was a principality of his own rather than the liberation of Jerusalem.',
    'Anna Komnene\'s famous, fascinated description of him — handsome, intimidating, and dangerous, with a faint, calculating smile — captures the mixture of admiration and distrust he inspired. His seizure of Antioch through guile, his maneuvering against rivals, and his later war on the Byzantine Empire itself confirmed the reputation: a leader of dazzling military skill and boundless, self-serving ambition.',
    'The portrait is consistent across friendly and hostile sources, which is itself telling: even his enemies admired his ability, and even his admirers noted his guile. He is remembered as the archetype of the crusading opportunist — magnetic, gifted, and relentlessly ambitious — who turned a holy war into the foundation of his own Norman principality.'
  ],
  'tancred-prince-of-galilee': [
    'Tancred is remembered as one of the boldest and most aggressive of the First Crusade\'s younger leaders, a Norman warrior whose energy and ambition made him both an effective commander and a frequent source of friction. The sources present a daring and impetuous fighter, nephew and follower of Bohemond, who pushed forward in the campaign\'s hardest moments and carved out territory for himself in the Holy Land, becoming a regent and prince in his own right.',
    'A later epic poem idealised him as a chivalric hero, but the contemporary record shows a more complicated figure: brave and capable, yet grasping and quarrelsome, ready to seize towns and dispute spoils with fellow crusaders. His ambition, like his uncle\'s, was directed firmly at his own advancement.',
    'What the record supports is a vigorous, courageous, and acquisitive commander, central to the military success of the crusade and to the consolidation of the Latin states. He is remembered as a daring and effective warrior-prince — chivalric in later legend, but in the contemporary sources an ambitious and combative figure typical of the Norman adventurers who shaped the crusader east.'
  ],
  'alexios-i-komnenos': [
    'Alexios I Komnenos is remembered with unusual intimacy because his daughter Anna Komnene wrote his biography, the Alexiad, which presents him as a brilliant, resourceful, and tirelessly cunning emperor who saved the Byzantine Empire from collapse. Through her admiring eyes he appears as a master of diplomacy and stratagem, a ruler who survived endless threats — Normans, Pechenegs, Turks, and the unpredictable crusaders — through guile, patience, and adaptability rather than brute force.',
    'Anna\'s portrait is deeply partisan, written by a devoted daughter to glorify her father and lament the throne she felt was stolen from her, so its admiration must be discounted accordingly. The crusader sources, by contrast, often saw the same shrewdness as treachery, presenting Alexios as slippery and untrustworthy — a clash of perspectives that reveals how his diplomatic subtlety could read as either wisdom or deceit.',
    'What both traditions agree on is the cunning: a ruler who preferred negotiation, bribery, and maneuver to open battle, and who rebuilt imperial power through political skill. He is remembered as the wily restorer of Byzantium — a calculating and resilient statesman whose intelligence is celebrated by his daughter and resented by the westerners who had to deal with him.'
  ],
  'anna-komnene': [
    'Anna Komnene is remembered as one of the most remarkable personalities of the Byzantine world, and unusually, her character emerges directly from her own great work, the Alexiad. She presents herself as deeply learned, proud of her education in philosophy, rhetoric, and the classics, and openly bitter about her exclusion from power after her plot to seize the throne for her husband failed and she was confined to a convent.',
    'That combination — formidable intellect, dynastic pride, and resentment at her thwarted ambition — runs through her writing. She is admiring and tender toward her father Alexios I, scornful of those she considered usurpers or barbarians, and candid about her own frustrated desire to rule. Her vivid, opinionated portraits of crusaders like Bohemond reveal a sharp, judgemental, and intensely engaged mind.',
    'Because we see her chiefly through her own self-presentation, the portrait is self-fashioned, shaped to vindicate herself and her father. But the consistency of the evidence — learning, pride, ambition, and lasting bitterness — makes Anna one of the most vividly recoverable individuals of the twelfth century, remembered as a brilliant, proud, and disappointed princess-historian.'
  ],
  'fulcher-of-chartres': [
    'Fulcher of Chartres is remembered through his own chronicle of the First Crusade and the early kingdom, and his personality emerges as that of a thoughtful, observant, and relatively measured eyewitness. As a priest who accompanied the crusade and settled in the east as a chaplain to Baldwin I, he wrote with a curiosity about the new world the crusaders had entered, recording with some wonder how western settlers were becoming, in his famous phrase, "easterners" who had put off their old identities.',
    'His tone is more reflective and less bloodthirsty than some crusade chroniclers, and he shows interest in the land, peoples, and even the natural marvels of the east, alongside the religious framework that shaped his understanding of the crusade as God\'s work. He could be credulous about wonders, but he aimed at honest report of what he had seen.',
    'What the record allows — drawn largely from his own writing — is the portrait of a curious, devout, and comparatively open-minded observer, more chronicler than warrior. He is remembered less for deeds than for vision: the reflective settler-priest whose account preserves not just the events but the strange experience of westerners remaking themselves in the crusader east.'
  ]
})

Object.assign(content, {
  'imad-ad-din-zengi': [
    'Imad ad-Din Zengi is remembered as a fierce, hard-driving warlord whose ruthlessness made him the first great Muslim counterforce to the crusader states. The Arabic sources present a commander of severe discipline and explosive temper — feared by his own troops as much as by his enemies — who held his army to harsh order and pursued power in Syria and the Jazira with relentless ambition.',
    'His capture of the crusader county of Edessa in 1144, the first major reversal of the First Crusade\'s conquests, made him a hero of the counter-crusade in later Muslim memory and provoked the Second Crusade in response. The sources admire his effectiveness while not concealing his cruelty; he was assassinated by his own servants, a fitting end for a ruler who governed through fear.',
    'The portrait is of a brutal but formidable founder of a dynasty, whose violence was inseparable from his success. He is remembered as the hard man who first showed that the crusaders could be rolled back — admired in the Muslim tradition for the blow he struck at Edessa, and acknowledged even by sympathetic sources as a commander as ruthless toward his own as toward his foes.'
  ],
  'nur-ad-din': [
    'Nur ad-Din made jihad rhetoric and institutional patronage central to his rule, and the Arabic sources remember him as a model of pious, disciplined, and just Islamic kingship — a marked contrast to his ferocious father Zengi. They present a ruler of personal austerity and devotion who built mosques, schools, and hospitals, cultivated the support of religious scholars, and framed the war against the crusaders as a sacred duty to recover Jerusalem.',
    'This favourable portrait owes much to writers who admired his religious legitimacy and his role in unifying Muslim Syria, so its emphasis on piety and justice is partly the image he and his supporters cultivated. Yet his achievements were real: he joined Aleppo and Damascus, pressed the crusader states hard, and laid the political and ideological groundwork that Saladin would inherit and complete.',
    'What the record consistently conveys is a ruler who combined genuine personal devotion with shrewd political and propagandistic skill, making the counter-crusade a matter of religious revival as well as conquest. He is remembered as the pious unifier — austere, disciplined, and strategically patient — whose fusion of faith and statecraft set the stage for the recovery of Jerusalem.'
  ],
  'shirkuh': [
    'Shirkuh is remembered as a bold, blunt, and physically formidable Kurdish commander, the uncle of Saladin whose campaigns in Egypt opened the path to Ayyubid power. The Arabic sources present a stout, one-eyed, hard-fighting general of great energy and ambition, renowned for his courage and his appetite, who served Nur ad-Din and led the difficult expeditions that drew his nephew into the contest for Egypt.',
    'He emerges as a soldier\'s soldier — direct, daring, and effective in the field — whose drive secured control of Egypt shortly before his death, which the sources attribute to his heavy eating and stout constitution. His brief tenure as vizier there set in motion the rise of his family.',
    'What the record supports is a vigorous and ambitious commander whose boldness created the opportunity that Saladin would seize. He is remembered as the rough, brave, and decisive general who carried Kurdish military power into Egypt — the uncle whose conquests made the Ayyubid future possible, even though he did not live to see it realised.'
  ],
  'al-adil-i': [
    'Al-Adil I, brother of Saladin, is remembered as a shrewd, patient, and supremely capable administrator and diplomat, the steady hand who held the Ayyubid realm together. Both Muslim and crusader sources — to the latter he was known as Saphadin — present a ruler whose talents lay less in battlefield glory than in governance, negotiation, and the careful management of a sprawling family confederation.',
    'He served as Saladin\'s indispensable lieutenant and then, after a period of skilful maneuvering among his nephews, secured supreme power for himself, governing with a pragmatism that favoured truces and trade with the crusader states over endless war. The sources credit him with political subtlety and financial acumen rather than the charisma or piety emphasised in his brother\'s legend.',
    'What the record conveys is a calculating and effective statesman who valued stability and consolidation — the organiser who turned Saladin\'s conquests into a durable dynastic structure. He is remembered as the patient diplomat-administrator of the Ayyubid house, whose quiet competence preserved and ordered what his more famous brother had won.'
  ],
  'al-kamil': [
    'Al-Kamil, the Ayyubid sultan of Egypt, is remembered for an extraordinary act of diplomacy that defied the logic of holy war: his negotiated surrender of Jerusalem to the excommunicated emperor Frederick II in 1229, achieved without a battle. The sources, Muslim and Christian alike, present a pragmatic and intelligent ruler who preferred negotiation to bloodshed and who startled both sides by treating with the crusader emperor as a fellow sovereign and near-equal in curiosity.',
    'His willingness to bargain away Jerusalem drew criticism in the Muslim world, but it reflected a calculating mind that valued Egypt\'s security over symbolic possession of the holy city. He is also remembered for his celebrated meeting with Francis of Assisi during the Fifth Crusade, an encounter that tradition presents as marked by courtesy and mutual respect.',
    'What the record supports is a sophisticated, flexible, and intellectually curious ruler, more interested in practical advantage and cultural exchange than in ideological rigidity. He is remembered as the sultan who gave up Jerusalem by treaty — a pragmatic and worldly statesman whose diplomacy with Frederick II remains one of the most surprising episodes of the crusading age.'
  ],
  'baybars': [
    'Baybars is remembered as one of the most formidable and ruthless rulers of the medieval Islamic world, a Mamluk sultan of terrifying energy who broke the back of both the Mongols and the crusader states. The Arabic sources present a commander of relentless drive, cunning, and severity — a former slave-soldier who rose through martial brilliance, played a decisive role in the victory over the Mongols at Ain Jalut, and then murdered his way to the throne.',
    'As sultan he was a tireless campaigner and a cold political operator: he dismantled crusader strongholds one by one, terrorised his enemies, built a centralised military state, and legitimised his usurped power by installing a puppet caliph in Cairo. The sources admire his effectiveness and discipline while recording the violence and treachery by which he ruled.',
    'What the record consistently conveys is a ruler of overwhelming will and ability, equally capable of strategic patience and sudden brutality. He is remembered as the great Mamluk warrior-sultan — ruthless, restless, and supremely competent — whose campaigns reshaped the Near East and sealed the long decline of the crusader presence in the Holy Land.'
  ],
  'qutuz': [
    'Qutuz is remembered through the single decisive achievement of his short reign: the Mamluk victory over the Mongols at the Battle of Ain Jalut in 1260, which halted the seemingly unstoppable Mongol advance. The Arabic sources present a resolute and courageous sultan who, facing an existential threat, rallied the forces of Egypt, rejected Mongol demands for submission with defiance, and led his army to a triumph that changed the course of history.',
    'His leadership in that crisis — including the famous moment when he is said to have thrown off his helmet and cried out to encourage his wavering troops — fixed his reputation for personal bravery and decisiveness. Yet his reign ended almost immediately in betrayal: he was assassinated soon after the victory, by tradition at the hands of his own commander Baybars, who took the throne.',
    'What the record supports is a brave and capable ruler whose defining moment was the salvation of the Muslim Near East from the Mongols, cut short by the ambition of a subordinate. He is remembered as the victor of Ain Jalut — resolute and courageous in the supreme crisis — whose triumph was swiftly followed by his murder.'
  ],
  'kilij-arslan-i': [
    'Kilij Arslan I is remembered as the young and energetic Seljuk sultan of Rum who confronted the First Crusade as it cut across Anatolia, and the sources — chiefly Byzantine and crusader — present a bold but ultimately outmatched adversary. He had earlier destroyed the disorganised People\'s Crusade with ease, which may have led him to underestimate the disciplined main army that followed, allowing it to take his capital and defeat him in the field.',
    'Recovering from these reverses, he proved a persistent and capable enemy of both the crusaders and the Byzantines, and later destroyed a subsequent crusading force, demonstrating that his early defeats had not broken him. The sources present a determined and adaptable ruler navigating the sudden, overwhelming pressure of the crusade and the rivalries among Muslim powers.',
    'Because he is seen mainly through the eyes of his enemies, his individual character is only partly visible. What the record supports is a brave, resilient, and ambitious ruler who misjudged the First Crusade but recovered to defend and expand his sultanate. He is remembered as the Seljuk sultan who first met the crusaders in Anatolia — formidable, persistent, and shaped by that collision.'
  ],
  'kerbogha': [
    'Kerbogha, the atabeg of Mosul, is remembered through his dramatic failure to crush the First Crusade at Antioch in 1098, and the sources — mostly crusader — frame him as the embodiment of overconfident Muslim power brought low. They present a proud and powerful commander who assembled a large coalition army to relieve Antioch, only to see the starving, desperate crusaders sally out and rout his force in one of the most improbable victories of the campaign.',
    'The Christian sources, eager to magnify a providential triumph, portrayed him as arrogant and boastful — even staging a scene of his mother warning him against attacking the Franks — so the portrait of haughtiness is partly literary, designed to make his defeat a lesson in pride punished. His failure owed much to the disunity of his coalition, whose emirs did not trust one another.',
    'What the record allows, behind the hostile framing, is a formidable but ultimately unsuccessful commander undone as much by the divisions within his own army as by the crusaders\' desperation. He is remembered chiefly as the general who should have destroyed the First Crusade and did not — a figure whose defeat the Christian sources turned into a parable of arrogance.'
  ]
})

Object.assign(content, {
  'bernard-of-clairvaux': [
    'Bernard of Clairvaux is remembered as the most powerful and compelling religious voice of his age, a Cistercian abbot whose eloquence, asceticism, and force of personality made him an arbiter of popes, kings, and crusades. His own voluminous letters and sermons, together with admiring contemporary accounts, present a figure of intense spiritual fervour and iron will — physically frail from extreme self-denial, yet capable of dominating councils and swaying multitudes with his preaching.',
    'His preaching of the Second Crusade drew enormous crowds, and his conviction could be ferocious: he pursued theological opponents such as Abelard relentlessly and brooked little compromise on matters he regarded as touching the faith. Admirers saw a saint of overwhelming holiness; others experienced an austere and domineering zealot who involved himself in every controversy of his time.',
    'When the Second Crusade he had championed collapsed, he was forced to defend his role, attributing the failure to the sins of the crusaders rather than to the venture itself. The portrait, drawn largely from his own writings and from devoted followers, is consistent: a brilliant, austere, and formidably willful churchman — saintly to his admirers, overbearing to his critics — whose personal authority shaped the religious life of twelfth-century Europe.'
  ],
  'louis-vii-of-france': [
    'Louis VII of France is remembered as a pious, well-meaning, and somewhat ineffectual king, whose monastic temperament sat awkwardly on a throne that demanded harder qualities. The sources present a ruler of genuine and conventional devotion — he had been intended for the church before his brother\'s death made him heir — whose religious scruples and mild nature contrasted with the political ruthlessness of his rivals and successors.',
    'His leadership of the Second Crusade was earnest but unsuccessful, and his marriage to Eleanor of Aquitaine ended in an annulment that proved a political disaster, since she promptly married Henry of Anjou and carried her vast lands into the Angevin camp. The chroniclers, even sympathetic ones, convey a king better suited to prayer than to the cynical contest of twelfth-century power politics.',
    'What the record supports is a decent, devout, and patient ruler whose virtues were personal rather than political, and who was repeatedly outmaneuvered by tougher opponents. He is remembered as the pious king whose gentleness and miscalculations — above all the loss of Eleanor and Aquitaine — shaped the long Capetian-Angevin struggle more through failure than through strength.'
  ],
  'conrad-iii-of-germany': [
    'Conrad III of Germany is remembered as an energetic but ultimately unsuccessful ruler, the first Hohenstaufen king whose reign was dominated by the rivalry with the Welf dynasty and by the failure of the Second Crusade. The sources present a brave and active king, capable in war, but unable to master the factional conflicts of the empire or to overcome the disasters that befell his crusade in Anatolia, where his army was shattered.',
    'His personal courage is well attested — he fought hard and endured the hardships of the failed expedition — but the campaign\'s collapse, through poor logistics and Turkish attacks, marked his reign as one of frustrated effort. He never managed to be crowned emperor, dying before he could secure the imperial dignity.',
    'What the record conveys is a vigorous and well-intentioned ruler defeated by circumstances and by the structural conflicts of his realm. He is remembered as a king of real martial energy but limited lasting achievement — brave in the field, but presiding over the failed crusade and the unresolved rivalries that he passed, along with the throne, to his far more formidable nephew Frederick I Barbarossa.'
  ],
  'melisende-of-jerusalem': [
    'Melisende of Jerusalem is remembered as one of the most powerful women of the crusader east, a queen who ruled in her own right and fought tenaciously to keep that power. The sources, notably the chronicler William of Tyre, present a capable, determined, and politically astute ruler who governed the Kingdom of Jerusalem as heir to her father Baldwin II, first alongside her husband and then in a bitter struggle against her own son when he sought to push her aside.',
    'William of Tyre admired her wisdom and capacity for rule even as the gendered assumptions of his age shaped his account, and the record shows a queen who commanded real loyalty among the nobility and clergy, secured key appointments, and was a notable patron of the church and the arts. Her conflict with her son Baldwin III, which nearly broke into open war, ended in a settlement that preserved her dignity and a continuing political role.',
    'What the record supports is a formidable and skilled ruler who exercised genuine authority in a world that rarely accepted female sovereignty easily. She is remembered as a strong, intelligent, and resilient queen — a woman who claimed and defended real power, and whose long influence shaped the crusader kingdom across two reigns.'
  ],
  'baldwin-iv-of-jerusalem': [
    'Baldwin IV of Jerusalem is remembered as one of the most poignant and admired figures of the crusader east: the "leper king" whose courage and resolve defied a disease that was slowly destroying him. The sources, above all William of Tyre, who had been his tutor, present a young ruler of remarkable intelligence, dignity, and bravery, who insisted on leading his kingdom and even his armies despite the progressive ravages of leprosy.',
    'His tutor\'s account is affectionate and admiring, recording how the boy\'s illness was first noticed and how the young king refused to be reduced by it — most strikingly in his personal leadership at the victory of Montgisard against Saladin while still in his teens. The sources convey a ruler of unusual strength of will, holding a fracturing kingdom together by sheer determination as his body failed.',
    'Because the fullest portrait comes from a devoted teacher, the admiration is heartfelt and partisan, but the core is well grounded in the documented record of his reign. He is remembered as a tragic and heroic figure — clear-minded, courageous, and dutiful — whose personal valour against both Saladin and his own disease made him one of the most sympathetic rulers of the crusading age.'
  ],
  'guy-of-lusignan': [
    'Guy of Lusignan is remembered, largely unkindly, as the king whose poor judgement led to the catastrophic loss of the kingdom at the Battle of Hattin in 1187. The sources present a handsome but politically lightweight noble who rose through marriage to Sibylla of Jerusalem, and whose indecision and susceptibility to bad counsel — especially that of the hawkish faction urging battle — are blamed for marching the army to destruction in the waterless hills before Hattin.',
    'The hostile tradition, shaped by the magnitude of the disaster and by his aristocratic rivals, may exaggerate his incapacity; he was placed in an impossible position by a divided kingdom and aggressive enemies. Yet even sympathetic accounts struggle to credit him with the strength a ruler in crisis needed, and his later loss of the throne and consolation with the lordship of Cyprus did little to rehabilitate him.',
    'What the record conveys is a man elevated by marriage beyond his political weight, remembered as well-meaning but weak and easily led. He is remembered chiefly as the king of Hattin — the ruler whose fatal decision lost Jerusalem — a figure of tragic inadequacy more than of malice, blamed by a tradition that needed someone to hold responsible for the great disaster.'
  ],
  'sibylla-of-jerusalem': [
    'Sibylla of Jerusalem is remembered for the fierce loyalty to her husband that placed Guy of Lusignan on the throne against the wishes of much of the nobility, and her character survives chiefly through that act of determined devotion. The sources present a queen who held the hereditary right to the crown and who, when pressed to set aside the unpopular Guy, is said to have agreed to be crowned alone — and then defiantly crowned her husband with her own hands.',
    'That episode, whether recorded with admiration or dismay, fixed her reputation for stubborn personal attachment over political calculation, a choice that her critics blamed for the disasters that followed. The chroniclers, writing in the shadow of Hattin and the fall of Jerusalem, tended to judge her loyalty as a costly weakness.',
    'What the record allows is the picture of a queen who valued her marriage above the counsel of her barons and who used her hereditary claim decisively, if controversially. She is remembered for that singular act of devotion — the crowning of Guy — which secured her husband\'s kingship and, in the eyes of the tradition, helped seal the kingdom\'s fate. Her own inner character beyond this remains lightly documented.'
  ],
  'raymond-iii-of-tripoli': [
    'Raymond III of Tripoli is remembered as the wise, experienced, and cautious elder statesman of the crusader kingdom in its final crisis, the leader whose prudent counsel was tragically ignored before Hattin. The sources, especially William of Tyre, who respected him, present a shrewd and capable noble — twice regent of the kingdom — who understood the realities of power in the east and argued against the reckless confrontation with Saladin that the hawkish faction demanded.',
    'His pragmatism, including a controversial truce with Saladin during the kingdom\'s internal divisions, exposed him to accusations of disloyalty from his rivals, and the tradition is split: admirers saw foresight, enemies saw treachery. At Hattin, the battle he had warned against, he survived a desperate charge but the kingdom did not, and he died soon after, broken by the catastrophe he had foreseen.',
    'What the record supports is an intelligent and far-sighted leader whose caution was vindicated by disaster but who could not prevent it. He is remembered as the prudent voice unheeded — a capable and politically subtle noble whose realism set him at odds with the militant faction, and whose warnings the kingdom ignored to its ruin.'
  ],
  'reynald-of-chatillon': [
    'Reynald of Châtillon is remembered as the great villain of the crusader east, a reckless and violent adventurer whose provocations helped bring on the disaster of Hattin. Both Muslim and Christian sources present a brutal, ambitious, and faithless figure — a man who tortured the Patriarch of Antioch, raided into the Arabian heartlands toward the holy cities of Islam, and broke truces by attacking Muslim caravans, outrages that gave Saladin both motive and justification for war.',
    'The Muslim sources regarded him with particular loathing, and the famous tradition of his death holds that Saladin executed him personally after Hattin, having sworn to kill him for his violations — sparing the captured king Guy but striking down Reynald with his own hand. Even the Christian sources, while sometimes admiring his boldness, could not defend his treacheries.',
    'What the record consistently conveys, across hostile traditions, is a figure of exceptional aggression and bad faith, whose provocations were as politically disastrous as they were personally daring. He is remembered as the crusader east\'s most notorious troublemaker — bold, cruel, and faithless — whose recklessness helped ignite the war that destroyed the kingdom.'
  ],
  'balian-of-ibelin': [
    'Balian of Ibelin is remembered as the steady and honourable noble who organised the desperate defence of Jerusalem after Hattin, and the sources — Christian and Muslim alike — present a figure of unusual dignity and good faith amid the collapse of the kingdom. With almost no knights left, he took charge of the nearly defenceless city, knighted its boys and townsmen to mount a defence, and then negotiated its surrender to Saladin on terms that spared the population.',
    'The tradition credits him with both courage and shrewd, humane negotiation: facing the certain fall of the city, he is said to have threatened to destroy its holy places and kill the Muslim captives if no terms were offered, then bargained hard to ransom as many inhabitants as possible. His conduct earned respect even from the Muslim side.',
    'What the record supports is a capable, principled, and pragmatic leader who salvaged lives from catastrophe. He is remembered as the defender of Jerusalem in 1187 — a noble of integrity and resolve who, when the kingdom\'s cause was lost, used courage and negotiation to soften the disaster, and whose honourable conduct stood out among the recriminations of the kingdom\'s fall.'
  ],
  'conrad-of-montferrat': [
    'Conrad of Montferrat is remembered as the resourceful and ruthlessly ambitious defender of Tyre, the leader whose energy saved the last major crusader foothold after Hattin and who then maneuvered relentlessly toward the crown of Jerusalem. The sources present a capable and determined commander who arrived at a fortuitous moment, organised the defence of Tyre against Saladin, and made himself indispensable — while pursuing his own claim to the throne with single-minded political guile.',
    'His rivalry with Guy of Lusignan and his maneuvering during the Third Crusade revealed a calculating and self-interested operator, admired for competence but distrusted for ambition. Just as he secured election as king of Jerusalem, he was assassinated in Tyre by the Assassins, in a killing whose sponsorship was much debated and variously blamed on his many enemies.',
    'What the record conveys is a brilliant, energetic, and politically ruthless leader whose ability was matched by his ambition. He is remembered as the savior of Tyre and the king-elect who never reigned — a formidable and divisive figure whose competence kept the crusader cause alive and whose mysterious murder removed one of its most capable, and most grasping, leaders.'
  ],
  'henry-ii-of-champagne': [
    'Henry II of Champagne is remembered as the compromise ruler who held the rump crusader kingdom together during and after the Third Crusade, a capable and diplomatic figure thrust into authority by circumstance. The sources present a respected noble — nephew to both Richard the Lionheart and Philip II of France — who, after the assassination of Conrad of Montferrat, married Conrad\'s widow and assumed the leadership of the kingdom, governing from Acre with a pragmatic, conciliatory style.',
    'He never formally took the title of king, and his rule depended on balancing the factions, the Italian merchant communities, and relations with both the crusaders and Saladin\'s successors. The chroniclers present a sensible and reasonably effective ruler in a difficult position, managing a much-reduced kingdom with tact rather than force.',
    'His reign ended abruptly and strangely, in a fatal fall from a window at Acre. What the record supports is a moderate, diplomatic, and dutiful leader who provided a measure of stability in a fractured polity. He is remembered as the accidental but competent ruler of the kingdom\'s difficult middle years — a conciliator who held things together until his sudden death.'
  ]
})

Object.assign(content, {
  'enrico-dandolo': [
    'Enrico Dandolo, the blind and aged doge of Venice, is remembered as one of the most extraordinary figures of the Fourth Crusade, a man of astonishing willpower and political cunning who bent the whole expedition to Venetian interests. The sources — including the crusader Geoffrey of Villehardouin, who admired him, and the Byzantine writers who loathed him — present a ruler of advanced age and failing sight whose energy, courage, and shrewdness dominated the crusade.',
    'His role in diverting the crusade first against the Christian city of Zara and then against Constantinople itself made him, in Byzantine and later eyes, the chief architect of one of the great betrayals of the age — though his motives were a hard-headed blend of Venetian commercial advantage, repayment of debts, and political calculation. Villehardouin records his startling personal bravery, leaping ashore at the assault on Constantinople despite his blindness and age.',
    'What the record consistently conveys is a leader of formidable intelligence, iron determination, and unsentimental pursuit of his city\'s interest. He is remembered as the blind doge who steered the Fourth Crusade to the sack of Constantinople — admired for sheer force of character, condemned for the catastrophe he helped engineer, and unmatched in his single-minded service of Venice.'
  ],
  'boniface-of-montferrat': [
    'Boniface of Montferrat is remembered as the chosen military leader of the Fourth Crusade, a noble of distinguished crusading lineage whose authority was steadily eclipsed by the Venetians and by the momentum of events he could not control. The sources present a capable and ambitious commander, well connected in both the west and the Byzantine world, who accepted leadership of the expedition but found himself maneuvered by Doge Enrico Dandolo and entangled in the fateful diversion to Constantinople.',
    'His own ambitions in the east were considerable — he had ties to the Byzantine imperial family and hoped to gain a realm of his own — and after the sack of Constantinople he secured the Kingdom of Thessalonica, pursuing his interests in the carved-up empire. The chroniclers present a brave and honourable figure, yet one whose leadership was more nominal than real once the Venetians set the agenda.',
    'What the record supports is an able and ambitious noble overtaken by forces stronger than himself, who salvaged a kingdom from the wreck of the crusade before dying in battle against the Bulgarians. He is remembered as the crusade\'s titular leader — competent and well-intentioned, but unable to master either the Venetians or the disastrous course of the expedition he nominally commanded.'
  ],
  'baldwin-i-latin-emperor': [
    'Baldwin I, Latin Emperor of Constantinople, is remembered as the pious and capable crusader noble whose election to the new throne after the sack of 1204 placed him at the head of a fragile and doomed Latin empire. The sources, including Villehardouin, present a brave, devout, and honourable count of Flanders, respected among his peers, who was chosen emperor over the more ambitious Boniface of Montferrat and who took up the impossible task of ruling a hostile, fractured land.',
    'His reign was brief and ended in catastrophe: captured by the Bulgarians at the Battle of Adrianople in 1205, he died in captivity under circumstances that remained obscure and gave rise to grim legends. The chroniclers present a worthy and earnest ruler overwhelmed by the realities of an empire built on conquest and surrounded by enemies.',
    'What the record supports is a courageous and conscientious figure thrust into a role beyond any individual\'s capacity to sustain. He is remembered as the first and ill-fated Latin emperor — pious, brave, and honourable, but the ruler of a conquest-state that his own crusade had created and that could not be held, his life ending in a foreign prison.'
  ],
  'pope-innocent-iii': [
    'Pope Innocent III is remembered as the most powerful and assertive pope of the Middle Ages, a ruler of formidable intellect, energy, and conviction who pushed papal authority to its medieval height. The sources and his own voluminous letters present a brilliant lawyer-theologian of immense ambition for the church, who intervened in the affairs of kings across Europe, launched crusades, and convened the great Fourth Lateran Council that reshaped Christian life.',
    'His personality combined sweeping vision with practical command: he conceived of the papacy as the arbiter of Christendom and acted on it relentlessly, humbling monarchs through interdict and excommunication, yet he was also capable of frustration and limited reach, as when the Fourth Crusade he had launched defied him and sacked Constantinople against his explicit prohibition. His authorisation of the Albigensian Crusade revealed a harder, coercive side of his zeal.',
    'What the record consistently conveys is a ruler of exceptional ability and overwhelming sense of mission — confident, learned, and determined to order the Christian world under papal direction. He is remembered as the architect of papal supremacy at its zenith: brilliant and commanding, but also capable of unleashing forces, from the Fourth Crusade to the war on heresy, that escaped or exceeded his control.'
  ],
  'frederick-ii-holy-roman-emperor': [
    'Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor, is remembered as one of the most dazzling and controversial rulers of the Middle Ages, a figure his contemporaries called Stupor Mundi, the "Wonder of the World." The sources present a ruler of extraordinary intellectual curiosity and range — fluent in many languages, fascinated by science, mathematics, falconry, and philosophy, patron of a brilliant Sicilian court, and author of a serious treatise on birds — combined with a cold, autocratic will and a deep cynicism that scandalised the pious.',
    'His relationship with the church was one of open warfare: repeatedly excommunicated, denounced by popes as a heretic and even as Antichrist, he nonetheless recovered Jerusalem by negotiation rather than battle during a crusade he undertook while excommunicate — a feat that baffled both sides. His rumoured religious skepticism, his harem, his menagerie, and his ruthless statecraft made him a figure of fascination and horror to contemporaries.',
    'Because the sources are so polarised — adoring in his Sicilian circle, demonising in the papal camp — the man is genuinely hard to pin down, and modern historians caution against both the romantic and the hostile extremes. What is clear is a ruler of restless brilliance, autocratic ambition, and intellectual independence rare in his age. He is remembered as the Wonder of the World — curious, calculating, and unorthodox — whose dazzling mind and contempt for convention set him apart from every other medieval emperor.'
  ],
  'louis-ix-of-france': [
    'Louis IX of France became Saint Louis, and his remembered character is shaped by a sanctity that was, unusually, well attested in his own lifetime by those who knew him. The intimate memoir of his friend Jean de Joinville presents a king of genuine and rigorous piety — given to prayer, fasting, and charity, washing the feet of the poor, and dispensing justice in person beneath an oak at Vincennes — combined with personal warmth, humour, and an unbending sense of moral duty.',
    'His devotion was real and severe, expressed in two crusades that both ended in failure and, on the first, in his own capture and ransom in Egypt; he died on the second, at Tunis. Joinville\'s affectionate portrait also records a king of conscience who could be harsh in matters of faith — stern toward blasphemy and hostile to the Jews of his realm — so the sanctity carried a hard edge that an honest account must include.',
    'Because the fullest portrait comes from a devoted friend and from the process that canonised him, the holiness is emphasised, yet Joinville\'s candour about the king\'s human qualities gives it unusual credibility. He is remembered as the model Christian king — just, devout, and personally humble, but also rigid and uncompromising in his faith — one of the few medieval rulers whose saintly reputation rests on the testimony of those who lived beside him.'
  ],
  'andrew-ii-of-hungary': [
    'Andrew II of Hungary is remembered as an ambitious but financially reckless king whose reign weakened the crown even as he pursued grand ventures abroad. The sources present a ruler of restless ambition who led the Fifth Crusade to the Holy Land — a campaign that achieved little — while at home his lavish grants of royal land and revenue to nobles and favourites depleted the monarchy and provoked a constitutional crisis.',
    'That crisis produced the Golden Bull of 1222, a charter of noble liberties often compared to England\'s Magna Carta, which his own overspending and the resulting baronial pressure forced upon him. The chroniclers present a king more interested in prestige and generosity than in the careful husbanding of royal resources, whose open-handedness undermined the authority he sought to display.',
    'What the record supports is an ambitious, prodigal, and ultimately weakening ruler, remembered as much for the concessions wrung from him as for his own initiatives. He is remembered as the crusading king whose financial improvidence and lavish grants forced the Golden Bull — a monarch whose ambitions abroad and generosity at home together eroded the strength of the Hungarian crown.'
  ],
  'john-of-brienne': [
    'John of Brienne is remembered as a seasoned and respected warrior whose long career carried him from minor French nobility to the crowns of Jerusalem and the Latin Empire of Constantinople. The sources present a brave and capable soldier of considerable physical presence and martial reputation, who became king of Jerusalem through marriage, led the military efforts of the Fifth Crusade in Egypt, and late in life took up the defence of the embattled Latin Empire.',
    'His career was marked by resilience and personal courage — the chroniclers especially praise his vigorous defence of Constantinople in old age, when he reportedly fought with the energy of a much younger man. He combined the qualities of a competent commander with the adaptability needed to navigate the shifting politics of the crusader world.',
    'What the record conveys is a durable, brave, and widely respected figure who rose remarkably far through ability and advantageous marriages. He is remembered as a warrior-king of the crusading east — steadfast, courageous, and capable — whose long life of soldiering culminated in the heroic defence of the Latin capital, a fitting close to a career built on martial reputation.'
  ],
  'jean-de-joinville': [
    'Jean de Joinville is remembered through the warm, vivid, and deeply personal memoir he wrote of his friend and king Louis IX, one of the most engaging works of medieval literature, and his own character shines through every page of it. He presents himself as a loyal but independent-minded noble — devoted to the king yet willing to disagree with him, frank about his own fears in battle, his reluctance to join the second crusade, and his attachment to his lands and people.',
    'His personality, as it emerges from his writing, is candid, humane, observant, and touched with humour: he records his own anxieties and comforts as honestly as the king\'s holiness, and his refusal to swear he would not abandon Louis if the king persisted in a course he thought unwise reveals a man of conscience and plain speaking. His affection for Louis is genuine but never blind.',
    'Because we know him chiefly through his own self-portrait, the picture is shaped by his telling, but its candour and self-deprecation give it credibility. He is remembered as the devoted, honest, and warmly human chronicler of Saint Louis — a noble whose own engaging personality, preserved in his memoir, makes him one of the most likeable individuals to leave a voice from the thirteenth century.'
  ],
  'charles-of-anjou': [
    'Charles of Anjou is remembered as a cold, austere, and overwhelmingly ambitious ruler, a man of relentless drive who built a Mediterranean empire and pursued grand designs with ruthless determination. The sources present a stern, hard-working, and humourless prince — brother of Saint Louis but utterly unlike him in temperament — who conquered the Kingdom of Sicily, executed his young rival Conradin, and dreamed of restoring the Latin Empire and dominating the eastern Mediterranean.',
    'His severity and the heavy hand of his rule provoked the famous uprising of the Sicilian Vespers in 1282, which shattered his ambitions and revealed how widely his harsh, tax-heavy administration was hated. The chroniclers present a ruler of formidable energy and discipline whose lack of warmth and political flexibility ultimately undid his grandest projects.',
    'What the record consistently conveys is a man of iron ambition and cold efficiency, admired for his capability and feared for his ruthlessness. He is remembered as the austere empire-builder of the central Mediterranean — tireless, calculating, and grim — whose overreaching ambition and harsh rule brought him both a crown and, in the Vespers, a humbling reversal.'
  ],
  'gerard-thom': [
    'Gerard Thom, the founder of the Knights Hospitaller, is remembered as the pious and capable organiser whose charitable hospital in Jerusalem grew into one of the great military-religious orders. The sources, mostly later and shaped by the order\'s own institutional memory, present him as a devout and dedicated figure who oversaw the care of pilgrims and the sick before the First Crusade and then expanded the work after the city\'s capture into a formal religious community.',
    'Because order histories were written to honour their founders and define institutional identity, the early biographical details are sparse and idealised, and the historical Gerard is glimpsed mainly through the institution he shaped rather than through individual anecdote. What the record supports is a capable administrator and genuinely charitable founder, credited with securing the privileges and endowments that set the Hospital on its path.',
    'The honest assessment is that his personality is largely subsumed into the foundation story of the order. He is remembered as the devout founder whose care for pilgrims and the sick laid the groundwork for the Hospitallers — a figure known through the durable institution he created rather than through a vividly documented individual character.'
  ],
  'raymond-du-puy': [
    'Raymond du Puy is remembered as the organiser who transformed the Knights Hospitaller from a charitable brotherhood into a military religious order, and the tradition presents him as a capable, disciplined, and far-sighted leader. As the first to hold the title of master after the founder Gerard, he is credited with giving the order its rule, its military character, and its institutional structure, combining the care of the sick with armed service in defence of the crusader states.',
    'Because the sources are largely the order\'s own institutional memory, the portrait is shaped to honour a formative leader, and individual anecdote is scarce. What the record supports is an effective administrator and organiser whose decisions set the long course of the Hospitallers as both a charitable and a fighting order, defining a model that would endure for centuries.',
    'The honest assessment is that he is remembered through his institutional achievement rather than through a richly documented personality. He is the leader who militarised the Hospital — disciplined, organising, and consequential — a figure whose character survives chiefly in the durable shape he gave to one of the great orders of the crusading age.'
  ],
  'hugh-de-payns': [
    'Hugh de Payns is remembered as the devout and determined founder of the Knights Templar, the French knight whose vision of a brotherhood combining monastic discipline with armed protection of pilgrims created an entirely new kind of institution. The sources present a pious and resolute figure who, with a small band of companions, established the order in Jerusalem and then traveled to Europe to win recognition, securing the crucial support of Bernard of Clairvaux and a formal rule.',
    'Because the order\'s early history is sparsely documented and later shaped by both reverence and, eventually, hostility, the individual man is hard to recover beyond his founding role. What the record supports is a knight of genuine religious conviction and organising determination, whose persistence in winning ecclesiastical and royal backing turned a tiny fellowship into a powerful and wealthy order.',
    'The honest assessment is that he is remembered chiefly through the institution he created rather than through personal anecdote. He is the founder who fused the knight and the monk — pious, dedicated, and quietly consequential — a figure whose character is glimpsed mainly in the bold and original conception of the Templar order that he brought into being.'
  ],
  'jacques-de-molay': [
    'Jacques de Molay is remembered as the last Grand Master of the Knights Templar, a figure of tragic dignity whose end came in the destruction of the order by Philip IV of France. The sources present a conventional, conservative leader — brave and devout but not especially subtle — who was caught utterly unprepared by the French king\'s sudden, coordinated arrests of the Templars in 1307 on charges of heresy and blasphemy that were almost certainly fabricated.',
    'Under torture he confessed, then publicly recanted his confession, choosing to die rather than uphold a lie against his order — a final act of defiance that fixed his memory. Burned at the stake in 1314, he is said by tradition to have summoned his persecutors, the king and the pope, to answer before God within the year, a legend that grew when both did soon die.',
    'What the record supports is a steadfast and ultimately courageous leader, out of his depth in the political trap that destroyed him but unwilling to betray his order at the end. He is remembered as the martyred last master of the Templars — neither a brilliant statesman nor a villain, but a brave and conventional man whose dignified defiance in the face of injustice became the order\'s tragic epitaph.'
  ],
  'hermann-von-salza': [
    'Hermann von Salza is remembered as the brilliant diplomat who raised the Teutonic Order to greatness, a Grand Master of exceptional political skill who turned a minor crusading brotherhood into a powerful state-building institution. The sources present a trusted and astute figure who won the confidence of both the emperor Frederick II and successive popes, mediating between the two during their bitter conflicts and using that unique standing to secure privileges, lands, and opportunities for his order.',
    'His statesmanship was the foundation of the order\'s future: he steered it toward the conquest and colonisation of Prussia, obtaining the imperial and papal charters that gave the Teutonic Knights their later dominion in the Baltic. The chroniclers present a leader valued above all for his diplomatic genius, tact, and ability to remain trusted by rival powers at odds with each other.',
    'What the record consistently conveys is a master of negotiation and long-term vision rather than a battlefield hero — patient, shrewd, and politically indispensable. He is remembered as the architect of the Teutonic Order\'s rise: the diplomat-statesman whose skill in navigating the great powers of his age secured the privileges and the Baltic mission that defined the order for centuries to come.'
  ],
  'rurik': [
    'Rurik\'s personal character is almost impossible to recover. The Primary Chronicle makes him function less as a psychologically vivid person and more as a dynastic starting point: the foreign Varangian ruler whose invited arrival explains the origins of later Rus\' authority. His "personality" in the tradition is therefore political rather than intimate — he is remembered as the founder, the accepted outsider, and the root from which Rurikid legitimacy grew.',
    'Because the chronicle was compiled long after the events it describes, and because his very historicity is debated, there is no reliable basis for attributing temperament or traits to him. What the tradition offers is not a man but a function: the progenitor whose name later dynasties needed at the head of their line. To invent a character for him would be to mistake dynastic myth for biography.',
    'The honest approach is to describe how the tradition uses him rather than who he was. Rurik is remembered as the legendary founder of the Rurikid dynasty — a figure of origins and legitimacy whose personal qualities the sources neither record nor allow us to reconstruct, and whose significance lies entirely in what later generations built upon his name.'
  ],
  'oleg-of-novgorod': [
    'Oleg of Novgorod is remembered in the Primary Chronicle through his epithet "the Wise" (Veshchiy), which points to a reputation for cunning and foresight rather than to a documented personality. The chronicle tradition presents him as a shrewd and decisive leader — the regent who seized Kyiv by a ruse, killing its rulers Askold and Dir after luring them out, and who extracted tribute from the Byzantines after a bold campaign against Constantinople.',
    'These stories characterise him as clever, ambitious, and theatrical — hanging his shield on the gates of Constantinople in triumph — but they belong to a chronicle written long after, shaped by oral tradition and legend. The famous tale of his death, foretold by a sorcerer and brought about by a snake from the skull of his own horse, marks how thoroughly his figure passed into legend, echoing motifs found in Norse saga.',
    'The honest approach is to treat his "wisdom" and cunning as the qualities the tradition assigned to a successful founder-ruler rather than as recoverable biography. He is remembered as Oleg the Wise — crafty, bold, and ringed with legend — a figure whose vivid chronicle persona reflects how early Rus\' tradition imagined the shrewd architect of its power more than any documented individual.'
  ],
  'igor-of-kiev': [
    'Igor of Kiev is remembered in the Primary Chronicle chiefly through the manner of his death, which the tradition presents as a lesson in the dangers of greed. The chronicle portrays him as a ruler whose appetite for tribute exceeded prudence: having collected tribute from the Drevlians, he returned to demand more, and they killed him for his overreaching — a story shaped to moralise about the limits of a ruler\'s exactions.',
    'Beyond this didactic episode, the chronicle gives him campaigns against Byzantium and the Pechenegs, presenting a ruler of conventional ambition whose reign is recorded more in events than in character. The account was written long after, and its vivid moral framing of his death — and of his widow Olga of Kiev\'s terrible revenge — belongs to chronicle tradition rather than to documented biography.',
    'The honest approach is to recognise that his "personality" in the sources is largely the function of a cautionary tale. He is remembered as the ruler killed by the Drevlians for excessive tribute — a figure whose character the chronicle sketches mainly to illustrate the fatal consequences of greed, and whose individual temperament beyond this moral frame the record does not allow us to recover.'
  ],
  'olga-of-kiev': [
    'Olga of Kiev is remembered as one of the most vivid personalities in the Primary Chronicle, a ruler the tradition presents as fearsomely intelligent, ruthless, and — after her conversion — saintly. The chronicle dwells on her elaborate, terrible revenge for the murder of her husband Igor of Kiev: she is said to have buried one Drevlian embassy alive, burned another, and finally destroyed their city by a cunning stratagem with fire carried by birds, a sequence of vengeance told with grim relish.',
    'These stories cast her as cunning, implacable, and politically formidable, and the same tradition then presents her transformation into a wise Christian ruler, the first of her dynasty to be baptised, outwitting even the Byzantine emperor in the chronicle\'s account of her conversion at Constantinople. The vengeance tales are clearly shaped by legend and folklore motif, while her Christianity made her a saint in later Orthodox memory.',
    'The honest approach holds both the legendary and the venerated Olga together, recognising that the lurid revenge belongs to chronicle storytelling and the sanctity to devotional memory. She is remembered as the cunning and ruthless avenger turned wise Christian matriarch — one of the most dramatically characterised figures of early Rus\' tradition, vivid precisely because the chronicle made her a study in both terrible vengeance and holy wisdom.'
  ]
})

// ── apply ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const ids = new Set(data.characters.map(c => c.id))
const missingFromData = Object.keys(content).filter(id => !ids.has(id))
if (missingFromData.length) {
  console.error('Content written for unknown ids:', missingFromData)
}

let inserted = 0
let replaced = 0
for (const character of data.characters) {
  const paragraphs = content[character.id]
  if (!paragraphs) continue
  if (!Array.isArray(character.contentSections)) character.contentSections = []

  const section = { title: SECTION_TITLE, paragraphs }
  const existingIndex = character.contentSections.findIndex(s => s.title === SECTION_TITLE)
  if (existingIndex >= 0) {
    character.contentSections[existingIndex] = section
    replaced++
    continue
  }

  const birthIndex = character.contentSections.findIndex(s => /^birth and early life$/i.test(s.title || ''))
  const insertAt = birthIndex >= 0 ? birthIndex + 1 : 1
  character.contentSections.splice(insertAt, 0, section)
  inserted++
}

const withSection = data.characters.filter(c => (c.contentSections || []).some(s => s.title === SECTION_TITLE)).length
const without = data.characters.filter(c => !(c.contentSections || []).some(s => s.title === SECTION_TITLE)).map(c => c.id)

fs.writeFileSync(dataPath, JSON.stringify(data, null, 2))
console.log(`Inserted: ${inserted}, replaced: ${replaced}`)
console.log(`Characters with section: ${withSection}/${data.characters.length}`)
if (without.length) {
  console.log(`STILL MISSING (${without.length}):`)
  without.forEach(id => console.log('   - ' + id))
}
